{"id":"lane-ssg-review-b","parent":"self-sigterm-guard","ord":"a2V","title":"review: pi #bug","done":"2026-09-01T10:18:45-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T10:08:20-04:00","worked":2445,"desc":"Two rounds, both approve. Round 1 at e3583b21: 0 MUST, 1 SHOULD, 2 NIT — it confirmed the subreaper finding against the `prctl(2)` contract and noticed what makes the author's probe honest (both rules scored against the SAME observed ppid, so no timing confound). Round 2 at f280399c: approve, 0 MUST, 0 SHOULD, 1 NIT.\n\nThe re-verdict answered the called-for attack by TRACING rather than assuming: gate #355's check on the spawn shape and run the new leg — the kid reparents to the armed subreaper, the gated check reads `getppid() === 1` as FALSE, and the kid keeps beating through the very race the check was written for, so the leg times out with `orphaned ppid=<reaper>` in the log. An attributable failure rather than an incidental one.\n\nIt also found the leg refuses its own FAKE-GREENS, which is the half a hollow pin would lack: a subreaper that did not arm throws at the `prctl=0` gate before the leg can pass on an ordinary machine, and if reparenting ever landed on init anyway the kill would arrive but `expect(orphanedAt).not.toBe(1)` fails it. So the leg can only be green when the kernel arranged subreaper-world AND the tie caught the race — the design pinned, not the fixture.\n\nAnd it checked the zombie discipline nobody asked about: `gone()` is signal-0, which a zombie answers like a live process, so the subreaper's own `waitpid` loop is what makes \"the kid exited\" honest here — and it cannot race the wrapper's reaping, different child sets.","after":["lane-ssg-refactor"],"created":"2026-08-31T21:05:55-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:18:45-04:00","custom":{"kolu-terminal":"ccabad3c","musts":"0","reviewer":"agent-pi","shoulds":"1"}}
{"id":"wake-scope-outlines","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"ag","title":"PR: the wake scope picker lists documents #bug","done":"2026-09-01T12:13:02-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T09:46:53-04:00","worked":8769,"desc":"The wake control's file picker offers every file the directory serves, documents included — the human's screenshot shows `2026-09-01.md` sitting between `Shivaraj.olai` and `Srid.olai`.\n\n**A document cannot be a scope.** The doorbell derives its claimed set from the `kolu-terminal` values on the un-done NODES of the scope file, mirrors resolved to targets. A `.md` has no nodes, so a conversation scoped to one watches an empty set forever — and the failure is silent in the worst possible way: no wake, no digest, and a HEARTBEAT still saying the watcher is alive. A person would read that as quiet-and-fine while their whole fleet was unsupervised. That is exactly the confusion the heartbeat was built to prevent, handed to them by the picker.\n\n**The set to offer is a ruling, not a suffix match.** `.olai` is the floor and the served set already knows each file's kind, so the picker should ask the set rather than match a string. Beyond that: `_olai/Trash.olai` is an outline WITH nodes (it is in the screenshot), but a trashed lane's terminal claim is history rather than live work; `Pins.olai`, `Properties.olai` and `Kolu.olai` are outlines that will never carry a lane. Offering all outlines is a defensible answer if the picker genuinely cannot know which carry lanes — offering a file kind that provably cannot work is not.\n\n**And the stored scope outlives this fix:** a conversation already pointed at a document needs a stated behaviour, not silence. The fault path has a shape to imitate — the doorbell already says once, and survives a restart, when its scope file is renamed or deleted.","created":"2026-09-01T09:46:44-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:56:10-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-grok","brief":"briefs/wake-scope-outlines.md","dispatched":"2026-09-01T09:47:00-04:00","from":"the human 2026-09-01 ~09:45, with a screenshot of the control offering 2026-09-01.md beside the outlines","kolu-terminal":"2b640706","merge":"merge-auto","odu-worktree":".worktrees/wake-scope-outlines","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/457","tier":"ordinary","timings":"implement 35m + refactor 28m (claude-opus) · review grok 18m over 3 rounds · address 29m (claude→grok mid-round) · merge-master 5m + ci 10m + evidence 20m (grok) · dispatch→merge 2h25m"}}
