{"id":"feat-orch","ord":"aI","title":"Orchestrator","desc":"The orchestrator, as a product of olai itself (the plan: [orchestrator.md](../../../brainstorming/orchestrator.md) rev 2 — olai is the harness ON TOP of kolu, never launches agents). The PR order is EDGES the board enforces (`set_doing` refuses a blocked phase), not prose. **The standing rule: each PR is complete and correct on its own the day it merges — nothing deferred; the phases sequence work, never split a feature into a broken half and its fix.** The Kolu child subtree groups the padi/fleet integration family.","created":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:56:30-04:00"}
{"id":"tool-elapsed-face","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aC","title":"PR: a tool call still running after a few seconds shows its elapsed time #feature","done":"2026-08-21T17:19:05-04:00","desc":"- Any tool frame still `pending`/`in_progress` after ~N seconds, while the turn is live, gets elapsed time on its rail — \"· 47s\", ticking.\n- **Agent-agnostic by construction**: reads only ACP's own tool-call status plus whether a turn is in flight — no adapter `_meta`, no tool names — so it works with any ACP agent, not just the pinned Claude adapter.\n- Covers Monitor watchers, slow greps, long builds — anything the wire honestly calls running — with no need to recognize the tool.\n- Pattern: a pure sibling of `chat/spawn.ts`'s `doingOf` (PR #214's precedent), drawn from the same `Transcript.tsx` hook, unit-testable without starting an agent. Must gate on the conversation being live for `spawn.ts`'s own reason: statuses are sticky, and a dead conversation must not keep a timer counting.\n- Honest limit: this shows only what the wire calls running. Background calls the adapter completes at launch (background Task/Bash, likely Monitor) stay invisible until the adapter keeps them open — that half is upstream (claude-agent-acp issue #865; PR #941 covers Agent/Task only).\n\n**pr** — the elapsed rail off ACP status + liveness alone, per-row wake rule, clock.ts consolidation; the ChatEntry union question filed as chatentry-kind-union from=#324","created":"2026-08-21T15:23:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T17:19:05-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-21, from the ACP subagent-display discussion","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/f4e657ba","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/324"}}
{"id":"quick-capture-door","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"aB","title":"PR: quick capture — POST /capture lands text in the inbox (Raycast, share sheet) #feature","done":"2026-08-21T21:13:59-04:00","desc":"One small HTTP endpoint makes olai capturable from anywhere on the tailnet; every client after that is somebody else's few lines.\n\n**Ruled with the human 2026-08-21 (question tool):** v1 is TEXT-ONLY · auth is the tailscale identity header alone · every capture lands in the inbox · file capture is the follow-up node (capture-files).\n\n**The door:** `POST /capture` takes title/text/url, implemented over the same ops layer as everything else — validation, the inbox convention (`_olai/Inbox.olai`), auto-commit. Attribution: `Tailscale-User-Login` is required and recorded (tailscale serve injects it; transport is tailnet-gated, no public exposure, no token, no secret in any client). Captures arrive dated, so they land on the day's journal page. No `target` parameter in v1 — refile in the app; additive later.\n\n**Clients:**\n- **Raycast (macOS)** — the mail case with no mail infrastructure: AppleScript asks Mail.app for the selected message (Message-ID, subject, sender), builds the `message://…` link, prompts for a comment, POSTs. The link IS the attachment — the mail stays in Mail; olai holds the pointer, the comment, and `from`/`message-id` props (dedup via `prop:message-id`).\n- **Apple Shortcuts (iOS + macOS share sheet)** — URLs, selected text; \"Get Article using Safari Reader\" gives a read-later pipeline (article text as the note or a .md document).\n- Later, same endpoint: browser bookmarklet, Android via the PWA's Web Share Target, curl from scripts.\n\n**Two riders in this PR (verified against the code):**\n1. `message:` joins the sanitize schema — sanitise.ts is rehype-sanitize's default plus one named addition, and the default's href protocols are http/https/mailto, so a `message://` link in a note is STRIPPED today. One named protocol addition; sanitise.test.ts holds it against the default.\n2. Pin that the router hands non-http(s) hrefs to the OS instead of routing them, so a click opens Mail.app.\n\n**Caveats on the record:** `message:` links are solid on macOS; on iOS third-party-composed ones don't always resolve — subject/sender in the note keep captures findable regardless; test once. The retired alternative: an IMAP-folder-polling sidecar — only worth revisiting if full-body/attachment archiving is ever wanted (the vault outliving the mailbox).\n\n**pr** — POST /capture as a fourth Writer over the ops layer, CSRF closed under the serve threat model (strict content-type + Sec-Fetch-Site, pinned with proxy-supplied identity), both message:// riders, the palette/pin race folded via resolving.ts","see":["inbox-under-olai-dir","inbox-count-placement"],"created":"2026-08-21T16:47:29-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T16:08:28-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-21, from the email-forwarding discussion","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/e9e62d23","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/327","superseded-by-354":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: POST /capture was RETIRED by #354 (\"POST /capture is gone\", running.md:294) — capture is now an /mcp verb reached by `olai surface --url … capture` (title+note); this node's body shape and Tailscale-User-Login requirement describe a door that no longer exists"}}
{"id":"duration-values","parent":"search","ord":"aD","title":"PR: the query grammar learns durations — created:1h, changed:30m (m/h/d/w) #feature","done":"2026-08-21T18:48:39-04:00","desc":"The stamps carry second precision; the operators bottom out at days. One new value kind fixes it, RULED as follows.\n\n**The grammar:** durations spelled `<n><unit>`, units `m` (minutes), `h` (hours), `d` (days), `w` (weeks) — and NO month/year units ever (kills the Gmail-vs-Jira `m` collision by fiat, minutes win; month/year recency is already words: `last-month..`, `2026`). Two readings, both via the ranges the grammar already owns:\n- bare duration = within the last N (`created:1h`, sugar for `created:1h..`) — the Workflowy `last-changed:1h` / Gmail `newer_than:` reading, the one the human's fingers assumed;\n- duration as a range end = the point now−N (`created:..1h` is older-than-an-hour; `changed:2h..30m` a window; ends mix freely with day-words: `created:yesterday..3h`). Inequalities stay spelled as ranges — no `>=`, no `now-`.\n\n**Uniform across all three operators** (`date:`/`created:`/`changed:`): one value grammar, one refusal text; day-granular fields compare at their own precision, so `date:1h` effectively selects done-instants — stated in docs, not special-cased.\n\n**Anchoring:** ask-time, as `today`/`last-week` already are; a live `created:1h` page drifts within the hour exactly as `created:today` already drifts across midnight — evaluated at ask and on every revision, NO re-ask timer in v1.\n\n**Examples:** `created:1h` · `changed:30m` · `created:..1h` (older than an hour) · `changed:2h..30m` · `created:1d` vs `created:today` (rolling 24h vs since midnight) · `changed:1w` vs `changed:this-week` (rolling vs Monday-anchored) · `-changed:1d` (untouched a day) · `is:todo changed:1h`.\n\n**One place implements it:** the value grammar at the dates.ts/filter.ts boundary in @olai/format — the one-matcher rule means the palette, header box, on-page filter, chat @ list, ops search and MCP all inherit simultaneously. **Moves with it:** the refusal/usage text in filter.ts (the popover), search.md, filter-in-place.md's operator table, the search_nodes tool description.\n\n**Deliberately out:** comparison operators, snapping/rounding (`@h`, `/d`), function forms (`ago()`, `now-`), natural language, month/year duration units.\n\nPrior art surveyed 2026-08-21 (eleven systems; Workflowy the nearest kin — `last-changed:1h/1d`, hours+days only, bare = within-last-N): full survey in this node's git history via the removed brainstorming/relative-time-queries.md.\n\n**pr** — durations at the one-matcher boundary, hot path cheaper than before the feature, the refusal popover now teaches the grammar the human was refused by this afternoon","created":"2026-08-21T17:21:32-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T18:48:39-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-21 — born from typing created:1h and being refused; the three design questions ruled via the question tool; the brainstorming doc folded into this node and removed on the human's order","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/f2251cce","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/328"}}
{"id":"feat-teams","ord":"aIV","title":"Teams","desc":"Using olai together over a tailnet: knowing who you are, choosing which agent you talk to, capture with attribution. Still to design when it hurts: several people writing on one branch (fetch, merge policy, whose-work-is-whose) — the old deferral was folded into this theme.","created":"2026-08-21T17:50:52-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:56:30-04:00"}
{"id":"opencode-chat","parent":"feat-teams","ord":"a0","title":"Opencode joins the chat: choose an agent per conversation","desc":"Design: brainstorming/opencode-chat.md (spike-verified against opencode 1.17.9 on this machine; rulings 2026-08-21).\n- Each conversation is bound to ONE agent, chosen when the chat is created (always ask). Group chat is out of scope, permanently.\n- The roster is auto-detected on the backend (PATH probe per known agent); an empty roster shows install instructions.\n- The header shows the agent's icon and name beside the model (bundled brand icons, generic fallback).\n- Two self-sufficient PRs below — nothing unused, nothing deferred.","created":"2026-08-21T17:50:52-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:55:58-04:00","custom":{"brainstorm":"brainstorming/opencode-chat.md","from":"the human 2026-08-21"}}
{"id":"opencode-pr1","parent":"opencode-chat","ord":"a0","title":"PR: fix the chat client's fragile ACP assumptions #feature","done":"2026-08-22T01:50:32-04:00","desc":"Bugs today against ANY ACP agent — opencode just happens to trigger them — so this PR doesn't mention opencode at all:\n- Receiving the same tool_call_update twice must not break anything (opencode sends exact duplicates).\n- Stop using a frame's title as the tool's name — the title changes over a call's life; pick the name once at announcement.\n- A session/prompt that answers with an error (instead of a normal stop) must not wedge the conversation.\n- A replayed session that opens with user_message_chunk and already-collapsed tool frames must render fine.\nTested with the scripted fake agent the e2e suite already uses. Useful even if opencode support never lands.\n\n**pr** — the five any-agent robustness fixes incl. the ruled refused face (Unopened.tsx/face.ts), each pin mutation-checked; the chat panel stops betting on one agent's habits","created":"2026-08-21T17:50:52-04:00","changed":"2026-08-22T01:50:32-04:00","custom":{"merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/cea1d914","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/331"}}
{"id":"opencode-pr2","parent":"opencode-pr1","ord":"a0","title":"PR: choosing an agent — roster detection, picker, header identity, the opencode leg #next #feature","done":"2026-08-22T01:50:32-04:00","desc":"One PR, because no piece of it works without the others:\n- The server detects which agents are installed (PATH probe) and sends the client the list.\n- A new chat always asks which agent; an empty list shows install instructions instead.\n- The header shows the chosen agent's icon and name beside the model.\n- A conversation remembers its agent (stored next to its session id), so reopening it talks to the right one.\n- The opencode interpretation file: tool names from the toolCallId prefix, MCP auto-allow matches `<server>_`, no steering (the composer says messages will queue), settings read from method responses. The fail-safe rule stays: nothing is ever approved by failing to recognize it.\n- A fake opencode-shaped agent joins the e2e suite so all of the above is pinned.\nDetails and verified wire facts: brainstorming/opencode-chat.md.\n\n**pr** — @olai/chat agents/ legs (claude, opencode) behind one interface, roster detection (OLAI_ACP_AGENT + PATH probe, OLAI_AGENT_PATH), picker when >1 agent, header mark+name, the note remembers the agent, fail-safe shared, turn model a set (turns.ts) — which found and fixed a real idle-over-a-live-turn bug; 3 assumptions for the human: ask only when >1 installed (narrows 'always ask'), choose-at-boot adopts, memory one conversation deep","after":["opencode-pr1"],"created":"2026-08-21T17:50:52-04:00","changed":"2026-08-22T01:50:32-04:00","custom":{"merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/3bf9ce9f","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/336"}}
{"id":"tailscale-identity","parent":"feat-teams","ord":"a1","title":"PR: the header shows who you are — a trusted identity header (tailscale serve, Caddy + OAuth), with gravatar #feature","done":"2026-08-21T20:50:31-04:00","desc":"- One configurable pair of trusted header names (login + optional email) tells olai who the signed-in person is; the reverse proxy in front injects them. Default wiring: tailscale serve's `Tailscale-User-Login`. The same config covers Caddy + GitHub OAuth (caddy-security's `X-Token-User-*` via `inject headers with claims`, or forward_auth → oauth2-proxy's `X-Auth-Request-User`/`-Email`) and Authelia/Pomerium (`Remote-User`/`Remote-Email`) — one feature, not one per proxy.\n- The header shows the person: gravatar from the hashed email (generic fallback when no email claim), name beside or on hover.\n- The same value is the attribution other features read — /capture (quick-capture-door's ruling) and, later, whose-work-is-whose in team use.\n- No header (direct access, dev) → no identity chip, nothing breaks.\n- **Trust rule, stated in the PR**: the header is only meaningful when the proxy is the only way in — olai bound to loopback/tailnet, and the proxy stripping client-supplied copies of the header. Document it beside the config.\n- **Probable kolu ask**: the app is a websocket, so the header must be read at upgrade time — kolu's `serveSurfaceApp` owns the upgrade and doesn't hand request headers to the app today; exposing selected headers to the connection context is likely a small upstream change. Plain HTTP endpoints (the future /capture) read headers directly, no kolu change.\n- Implementation note: gravatar is a remote image fetch from gravatar.com — make sure the app page allows that image origin.\n\n**pr** — (by the human) — @olai/identity, GET /olai/who, the chip with honest absence, one config for Tailscale/Caddy/Authelia; the kolu websocket ask lives on as kolu-upgrade-headers in the Inbox","created":"2026-08-21T17:50:52-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T20:50:31-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-21","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/c4366b9b","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/330"}}
{"id":"identity-avatar-ladder","parent":"feat-teams","ord":"a2","title":"PR: the identity chip stops assuming the login is an email — real avatars for Tailscale and GitHub #next #feature","done":"2026-08-22T01:02:29-04:00","desc":"Follow-up to #330, from its first real deployment.\n\n**The motivating case:** a GitHub-backed tailnet's `Tailscale-User-Login` is `srid@github` — Tailscale's own spelling, correct to display, but NOT an email. #330 assumes login = email (\"that header is the email\", docs/running.md), so the gravatar hashes a non-address and every GitHub- or passkey-backed tailscale account gets the generic silhouette forever.\n\n**Fix — a picture resolution ladder, one config family, no provider hardcoding:**\n1. A **picture header**, when the proxy sends one (new optional `OLAI_IDENTITY_PICTURE_HEADER`; tailscale serve injects `Tailscale-User-Profile-Pic` — the IdP's real avatar, e.g. the GitHub one). Also read `Tailscale-User-Name` (display name) via an optional name header.\n2. An **avatar URL template** (`OLAI_IDENTITY_AVATAR_TEMPLATE`, e.g. `https://github.com/{login}.png`) — this is the Caddy + GitHub OAuth answer: oauth2-proxy's `X-Auth-Request-User` is the GitHub username, and GitHub serves every user's avatar unauthenticated at that URL. No API, no token.\n3. **Gravatar from the email claim** — only when the value actually looks like an email; #330's behavior, demoted to fallback.\n4. The generic silhouette.\n\n**Also in this PR:** correct docs/running.md's login-is-the-email claim (true for Google/Microsoft/Okta tailnets, false for GitHub/passkey ones), and the per-proxy wiring table gains the picture/name/template columns.\n\n**pr** — picture ladder (picture header → avatar template → gravatar-if-email → silhouette) resolved server-side, name header, /olai/who {login,name,picture}, img-src 'self' blob: https:, running.md corrected + proxy table; 3 assumptions for the human: Tailscale default header names (non-tailscale proxies must empty them — documented), https:-only rungs, tooltip name+login","see":["tailscale-identity"],"created":"2026-08-21T20:59:33-04:00","changed":"2026-08-22T01:02:29-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-21, on deploying #330 (the srid@github confusion)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/3d45ff08","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/337"}}
{"id":"vault-level-settings","parent":"feat-teams","ord":"a3","title":"PR: CLI/nix can pin git policy — the Prefs toggles freeze read-only when it does #next #feature","done":"2026-08-21T23:45:41-04:00","desc":"**The problem:** in a team deployment, auto-commit/auto-push must be the server's decision, the same for everyone — not whichever browser's pref happens to be set. Single-user, today's browser prefs are fine and stay.\n\n**Ruled 2026-08-21 (refined the same day):**\n- **Default: unchanged.** With no flag given, \"Git commit\" and \"Git push\" remain live browser prefs exactly as they are today.\n- **The override: CLI flags** (`--commit`, `--push`), surfaced as home-manager module options. When a flag is given, the server tells the client, and the Prefs toggles render in the pinned state, **read-only, with the source named** (\"set by the server: --commit=auto\"). Never hidden, never overridable from a browser.\n- **No vault settings file** (the earlier settings.json / Settings.olai brainstorm is dissolved): policy is a property of the running instance, and a file in the vault would make a personal clone inherit the team's auto-push via `git pull` — exactly wrong.\n- The refusal-pause behavior survives under a pin as runtime STATE: a refused commit/push still pauses the loop, and the frozen section carries a **Resume** button (today's off-and-back-on dance needs a live toggle, which a pinned section no longer has).\n- Theme/font/size/notes/done/hidden-outlines are untouched — personal view choices, per-browser.\n\n**Ships:** the two flags + HM options, the pin traveling to the client, the frozen faces with their sentence, Resume, docs/running.md.\n\n**pr** — --commit/--push as a pin on the git cell, Prefs rows frozen with the flag named, Resume under a pin, HM services.olai.commit/.push; 3 assumptions for the human: --push governs browsers only (no server-side push), --commit auto→on/manual→off/off→off, --no-commit pins as --commit=off","created":"2026-08-21T21:03:26-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T23:45:41-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-21, from the Prefs screenshot review","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/524e9086","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/335"}}
{"id":"one-search-box","parent":"search","ord":"aE","title":"PR: one search box — in the HEADER on every page, scoped to the page by a deletable \"This page\" chip, searching everywhere (/search?q=…) without it; the page filter bar and ⌘K's node hits go #next #feature","todo":true,"desc":"**The complaint (2026-08-21):** `#next` was tagged on five nodes in three outlines and nothing in the app lists them together. The filter box, `?q=`, and clicking a tag all narrow ONE page; the only directory-wide doors (header box, ⌘K) are shortlists for jumping TO a node — capped at 8, no address, not pinnable. Two entry points, two scopes, two answer shapes, one grammar.\n\n**Ruled (question tool, 2026-08-21):**\n1. **One box survives: the page's filter box.** It already owns the address (`?q=`), the honest \"N of M\" count, ancestry-kept rows, the refusal line and pins. The header's search box is deleted; on the phone the magnifier focuses the one box.\n2. **Page first, widen explicitly.** Typing narrows the page in front of you exactly as today. The bar gains the missing truth beside \"3 of 41\": how many matched **elsewhere in the directory** (\"· 12 more in other files — search everywhere\"), and that line is the door that widens the SAME query. A shortcut for it is welcome (design to pick — e.g. Enter with nothing selected, or a scope toggle on the bar); the query text is never retyped.\n3. **Everywhere is a real page: `/search?q=…`** — a computed page beside /today, /agenda, /trash in routes.ts (spells no file; the one-page rule and `is:trashed` apply as on any door). Rows grouped by file, each with its ancestry kept and its reason lit (the filter's \"every row says why\"), uncapped, with its own \"N matches in K files\" count. Addressable, back-button, and **pinnable like any `?q=` page** — so a saved cross-vault search is a pin (`/search?q=%23next`). From a row, Enter/click goes to the node; the page is the thing the shortlist could never be.\n4. **⌘K focuses the one box.** The palette keeps its commands, the page's verbs and the pin row; its node-hit half goes — a non-command query hands off to the one box (on the current page, with the widen line). The `LIMIT = 8` shortlist (`search/Shortlist.tsx`, `count.ts`) and `HeaderSearch.tsx` are deleted, not kept as a preview; the composer's `@` list (chat) keeps its own bounded shortlist — it names one node into a message and is not a search door.\n\n**Consequences to carry, not re-decide:** one matcher still (`@olai/format` parseFilter/matching; the server answers every door — the page asks `Query.search` uncapped-or-paged, the in-place narrowing keeps riding the page's revision pulse per filter-rides-the-page.md). The \"more elsewhere\" number must be the wire's uncapped `total` minus the page's matches, or a second ask with no `file`/`under` scope — never a browser walk (vault-in-browser). Documents are hits on the everywhere page (search.md \"…and documents\"); they are not rows on a page filter today and that stays.\n\n**Design first, in the PR:** a short `docs/brainstorming/one-search-box.md` (the widen gesture, the row shape on /search, what the bar says at each scope, mobile) lands as the PR's first commit for the human to read before the code; docs/search.md's \"five doors\" paragraph becomes the one-door story.\n\n**pr** — CLOSED unmerged 2026-08-22 on the human's word — the UI to be re-evaluated later; branch one-search-box on origin holds both rounds (page-filter design at acdb4ee; header-box + This-page-chip round after)","created":"2026-08-21T21:48:57-04:00","changed":"2026-08-22T08:35:08-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-21 — tagged #next across three files and found no page that shows them all; the four rulings taken by the question tool the same evening","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/334","ruled-2026-08-22":"THE HUMAN, on reviewing #334 (~07:55): 'The one search box should be in the HEADER. Simple as. Exists in all pages. On .olai pages, it gets a \"This page\" thing by default (which the user can delete) to filter.' — this supersedes the earlier reading that the page's filter box survives; the box is the header's, on every page, with scope as a deletable chip; deleting the chip searches everywhere (/search?q=), re-adding it scopes to the page; ⌘K focuses that box. #334 is being rebuilt to it (lane-osb, address round 2).","ruled-2026-08-22-cmdk":"THE HUMAN ~08:37: 'I don't want to lose the Cmd+K palette UI' — the ⌘K palette stays as on master (commands, verbs, pins; its handoff rows into the one box are fine); ⌘K is NOT rebound to the header box. Supersedes the 'focuses the one box' reading of ruling 4 and the orchestrator's round-2 brief item 4.","status-2026-08-22":"back to todo; no design is ruled — the human re-evaluates the UI; what IS settled: one matcher, /search?q= as a real page was built and reviewed clean, the ⌘K palette UI stays","touches":"web/src/client/filter (FilterBar, narrowed, count), search (HeaderSearch, Shortlist, nodes LIMIT=8, count), palette (items, Palette), routes.ts (a computed page), pins; docs/search.md, editing.md; website"}}
{"id":"mcp-roster-visible","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aD","title":"PR: the chat shows which MCP servers a conversation has, not just the failures #feature","done":"2026-08-23T20:27:17-04:00","desc":"- Pull request #140 (the mcp-fail-visible item) shows MCP server FAILURES in a strip, and deliberately shows nothing at all on a healthy session. But \"which servers does this conversation have?\" is a question people actually ask — and the incident that prompted this was the model answering it wrong: opencode listed olai and deepwiki, omitted kolu, and then successfully called `kolu_lifecycle_create`. The user interface should answer that question, not the model.\n\n**What to draw, being honest about what each layer can know:**\n- **Servers handed over by olai** (fully knowable, and already computed by #140's probe and the `servers` event on the chat cell): `olai` (its own HTTP route, alive by construction) and `kolu` (probe-verified when the session opens). Draw them as chips with a dot — `olai ✓ · kolu ✓` — in the chat header's popover, or generalize #140's strip into the full roster with per-server state, keeping the failure sentences word for word.\n- **Servers actually connected, where the agent says so:** the Claude adapter's system-init message carries per-MCP-server connection status — one more field read from the same message the live model name already comes from. This only works for the Claude leg.\n- **The agent's own servers** (for example, deepwiki configured in the user's opencode.json) are invisible to olai by construction — say \"plus the agent's own\", never pretend the list is complete.\n\n**Scope note:** this display is per conversation, because servers are handed over when the session opens; the roster row lives with the agent identity the header already shows.\n\n**Shipped 2026-08-23 at 20:26 on the human's approval.** The roster is built from the literal `mcpServers` array; \"connected\" status is minted only from the Claude leg's init message, using its own wording and guarded by session id; the agent's own servers honestly get no rows, sitting behind the phrase \"plus the agent's own\" (both reviewers engaged with this and agreed); a refused or departed session clears the strip; and #140's failure sentences are intact. The macOS continuous-integration lane was fully green on petit (the quiet macOS build machine); the Linux lane was red only on the legs that flake under load, which was filed in the Inbox. Evidence: six images plus a before-and-after pair of the unopened state.","see":["mcp-fail-visible"],"created":"2026-08-22T12:30:24-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-22 — asked opencode \"what MCPs do you have\" and it omitted kolu, then used kolu fine","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/4108cf9b","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/357"}}
{"id":"git-policy-server-side","parent":"feat-git","ord":"a4","title":"Git policy moves to the server: Auto-commit's quiet-window loop, Auto-push, the pause and the outcome are facts about the directory, not one tab's memory #now #feature","done":"2026-08-22T17:20:31-04:00","desc":"**Today:** Auto-commit (#301) is a 15 s quiet-window loop in the browser (commit/auto.ts, flurry.ts) behind a per-browser Web-Locks leader (commit/elected.ts); Auto-push (#283) fires only after THIS tab's commit (commit/record.ts:26); the pause-on-refusal (commit/pause.ts) and the last commit/push outcome (commit/state.ts) are tab memory. The server's `--commit=auto` is a different thing (one commit per write, no window, never pushed — `gitPolicy.ts:82`). #335 called the browser-preference shape \"exactly wrong for a team\" and answered by PINNING the toggles, not by moving the loop.\n\n**Ruled (the human):** git policy is a fact about the DIRECTORY. The loop lives on the server (one per served directory; quiet window over the server's own `pending`, which MCP/capture/vim writes move too); `--push=auto` pushes after each settled commit — the server's own included; the refusal, the pause and the last push outcome are fields of the git cell, drawn on the chip (⚠ with git's words, never `✓ committed` over a failing push) and identical in every tab; the prefs toggles set the server's policy through a procedure, persisted outside the vault (no settings file that travels with `git pull` — #335 stands), pinned by the flags when given; the tab loop, leader lock, tab pause and per-tab outcome signals are deleted.\n\n**Why browser-side happened:** the quiet window was framed as \"the person stopped typing\" — a claim about the reader — and shipped as a prefs row beside theme; nobody built the server-side window. The other browser-side residents are fine (theme, density, Done-visibility, panel geometry, folds, undo, chat drafts) — the git family is the whole of the nonsense.\n\n**pr** — merged 2026-08-22 as bec4334c","created":"2026-08-22T13:36:11-04:00","changed":"2026-08-22T17:20:31-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-22 13:35, after the push-failure RCA: 'why was auto-commit browser-side? any other nonsense riding browser-side?' — ruled: move it server-side, dispatch to Opus, auto-merge on verification","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/349","ruled-restart":"the human 17:05: remember nothing across a restart; under --push=auto with unpushed commits, one push on boot re-earns the words; nothing under manual/off"}}
{"id":"push-failure-invisible","parent":"git-policy-server-side","ord":"a0","title":"A refused push is invisible: the chip says \"✓ committed · 13 unpushed\" and nothing tells you why — the push outcome lives only in one tab's memory #now #feature","done":"2026-08-22T17:20:30-04:00","desc":"**Symptom:** auto-push on; the header chip reads `✓ committed · 1h ago · 13 unpushed`, the popover \"13 commits not on origin/main · Push\" — and no error anywhere.\n\n**Root cause (two facts, both by design today):**\n1. **Auto-push is a browser behaviour, not a server one.** The only trigger is `packages/web/src/client/commit/record.ts:26` — `if (autoPush && tag === \"Committed\") push()` — inside the success callback of THIS tab's own `git.commit` (Commit button or this tab's Auto-commit loop). Commits made by `--commit=auto` on the server, by an agent's `commit` tool, or by `POST /capture` are never pushed (`packages/ops/src/pending.ts:819-891` ends without a push; `docs/running.md:207`, `docs/git.md:95` say so). So \"unpushed\" grows whenever the writer was not a browser tab.\n2. **A push refusal is never stored.** `packages/git/src/git.ts:803` runs a bare `git push` (no hard-coded branch — `origin/main` is parsed from `git status --branch`, `git.ts:509-517`; `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` + 10 s budget, so no hang); a refusal is `Effect.logWarning(\"olai git: the branch was not pushed\", said)` and returned to the one caller (`pending.ts:785-790`), which does NOT call `settled()` / `onRecorded` — so the `git` cell (`GitState = {status, said, pinned}`, `packages/format/src/committing.ts:142-165`) has no push-error field and is not republished. The only place the words exist is the per-tab signal `pushed` (`commit/state.ts:132`), cleared on the next attempt (`:142`), lost on reload, drawn only inside the popover (`Unpushed.tsx:62-68`), never on the chip (`said.ts:289` has no push arm). Auto-commit's pause on a failed push (`auto.ts:194-198`) is also in-memory per tab.\n\n**Other ways it reads green while failing:** the server pin `--push=off` silently overrides the browser toggle (`settings/autopush.ts:36`; the prefs row shows \"Set by the server\" — easy to miss); `--commit=off` disables push too (`pending.ts:764`); a non-fast-forward because origin/main moved (olai never pulls/rebases) refuses every push forever.\n\n**Fix (the seam):**\n- `pending.ts`: keep a `pushRefusal` beside `refusal` (`:424`), set on the Failed arm (`:786`), cleared on a successful push (`:792`), and call `options.onRecorded?.()` on failure too so the cell republishes.\n- `format/committing.ts`: `GitState` gains `pushSaid: NullOr(String)` (`sameGit` is schema-derived, picks it up).\n- Chip: `said.ts:324-327` `alsoUnpushed` says `· 13 unpushed — the last push was refused: <said>` when set, and `faceOf`/`Commit.tsx:117-120` shows a ⚠ face instead of `✓ committed` while a push is failing; the popover reads the cell, not only the tab-local signal.\n- And decide the first fact: should `--push=auto` also push the server's own `--commit=auto` commits (one network round trip per quiet window, not per write)? Today's docs say no; the symptom says a user with auto-push on expects yes.\n\n**Confirm on an instance:** `journalctl --user -u olai --since -3h | grep 'olai git:'` — `the branch was not pushed` + git's stderr is the only durable record; its absence means no push was attempted. `git log origin/main..HEAD --format='%s%n%(trailers:key=X-Olai-Writer)'` shows who made the 13.\n\n**pr** — merged 2026-08-22 as bec4334c (pushSaid on the git cell; the chip drops ✓ and carries git's words; a boot push under --push=auto re-earns the words after a restart)","created":"2026-08-22T13:24:58-04:00","changed":"2026-08-22T17:20:30-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-22 13:20 (screenshot: committed 1h ago · 13 unpushed on origin/main, auto-push on, no feedback); RCA by the orchestrator's explorer","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/349"}}
{"id":"surface-cli","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"aD","title":"`olai surface`: a CLI for every server verb, over HTTP #feature","done":"2026-08-23T16:44:01-04:00","desc":"**What this is.** One command, `olai surface <verb>`, that can call any verb the server offers (the same verbs MCP agents see, same names, same answers) from a terminal or a script. The generic part lives in kolu (`@kolu/surface-cli`, merged as kolu#2205); olai adds its verbs, the `capture` verb, and how to reach a running olai.\n\n**Decided 2026-08-23 (replaces the unix-socket design of #352, reverted):**\n- The CLI reaches the server over **HTTP**, exactly as `/mcp` does. No unix socket. `--url <server>` is required on every call — no default, no lookup.\n- Auth: the `/mcp` bearer locally; the reverse proxy's identity header remotely. `captured-by` = that identity header, or omitted. A caller can never set it.\n- A write prints one line — `captured into <vault> — <link>`; `--json` prints the full answer, which names the vault (root path + server URL). No magic on TTY.\n- `capture` takes a title and a note, nothing else, for now.\n- `olai surface` prints friendly help; the help is the doc.\n\n**Still true from 2026-08-22:** no phone door (phones use the web page); flat verb names identical to MCP; no `olai mcp` subcommand; `capture` is one entry in the TOOLS table so the CLI verb and the MCP tool are the same line; `POST /capture` goes once `olai surface --url … capture` is proven against the human's real vault.\n\n**History.** #352 shipped this over a per-user unix socket — unapproved; two servers on one machine raced for it and the human's capture landed in this repo's docs vault; a vault in a container behind HTTPS was unreachable. Merged without approval 2026-08-23, reverted the same day (#353), re-opened as draft #354. Nothing here merges without the human's explicit approval.\n\nDesign doc: brainstorming/surface-cli.md (rev 2 — to be revised to HTTP).","see":["quick-capture-door","surface-mcp"],"created":"2026-08-22T15:49:19-04:00","changed":"2026-08-23T16:44:01-04:00","custom":{"brainstorm":"brainstorming/surface-cli.md — rev 2 carries the rulings: Effect CLI assumed, no phone door, captured-by = what the door has, flat names, no olai mcp","from":"the human 2026-08-22, after /capture (#327): 'bad solution — we need a surface CLI client that can call any surface verb; just like surface-mcp, upstream surface-cli'","post-merge-bugs":"2026-08-23 12:05-12:20, the human on master after #352 (merged by the orchestrator without the human's approval): six child nodes (docs, wrong-vault, remote-capture, runtime-dir-flood, socket-mode-claim, exit-0-no-root). 12:18 RULED: surface.sock was an unapproved decision — #352 REVERTED (#353 = df841d7c, 12:25), re-opened as draft #354 from the same head for the follow-up; NOTHING in this area merges without the human's explicit approval.","shipped":"kolu#2205 merged 7a004a82 (2026-08-22); olai #352 merged 441b8f74 without approval and REVERTED (#353); now open: draft olai #354 + kolu#2207 (cli-override) — both to be taken to green with everything under this node, both approved by the human before merging"}}
{"id":"surface-cli-kolu","parent":"surface-cli","ord":"a0","title":"PR (kolu): `@kolu/surface-cli` #feature","done":"2026-08-22T22:08:42-04:00","desc":"Three commits, each of which stands alone:\n\n1. **The move, with no behaviour change:** `packages/surface/src/verbs.ts` now exports `SurfaceVerb`, `toolName` and `toInputSchema`. The surface-mcp package re-exports the old names, and its tests are unchanged and still green, which proves nothing moved behaviourally.\n\n2. **The new `packages/surface-cli`:** it exports `surfaceCommands`, `flagsOf` and `EXIT`, and adds no new dependency. Its tests drive the fixture surface from the README (load / processes / proc.kill) end to end over a unix socket, covering a golden `--help` output, every flag shape, the get/keys/watch verbs as newline-delimited JSON, the matrix of exit codes, and Ctrl-C during `--follow`. kolu-cli is deliberately NOT migrated in this pull request.\n\n3. **Documentation:** a website page `surface/expose-to-cli.mdx` plus a `ref-surface-cli` reference page, a package README, and a paragraph in expose.ts about which faces take one now naming the CLI.\n\nContinuous integration was run per the odu skill's instructions, on Linux; the merged commit hash was noted so olai could pin to it.\n\n**Merged into kolu on 2026-08-22 as commit 7a004a82.** It went through kolu's pre-merge quality routine and its continuous-integration run — the Linux lane on localhost, the macOS lane on the machine called petit — and comprised 31 commits across the three parts.","created":"2026-08-22T15:49:19-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2205"}}
{"id":"surface-cli-olai","parent":"surface-cli","ord":"a1","title":"PR #354: the CLI talks HTTP to the server named by `--url` (required); the unix socket goes #feature","done":"2026-08-23T16:43:50-04:00","desc":"Change on #354: replace the unix-socket transport with HTTP. Every `olai surface` call takes `--url <server>` — required, no default, no lookup of a remembered URL. The CLI is an MCP client of that server's `/mcp` (ruled 2026-08-23 12:52): one door, no new route; auth as `/mcp` clients do (loopback, else the bearer; through a reverse proxy the identity header). Streaming verbs (`get --follow`, `watch`) are not offered — `/mcp` is POST-only, accepted. `captured-by` = the identity header, else omitted; never caller-set. Delete `surface.sock`, `serveOverUnixSocket`, `--socket`, the dial ladder and their tests. POST /capture is deleted only after `olai surface --url https://myolai… capture` is shown working against the human's real vault (transcript in the PR).\n\nWhy: #352 used one unix socket per user — two servers on a machine raced for it and the human's capture landed in the wrong vault; a vault in a container behind HTTPS was unreachable.\n\n**pr** — merged 2026-08-23 as 6a863f38 on the human's approval (CI green at the approved head e88e97c4; the video verified)","after":["surface-cli-kolu"],"created":"2026-08-22T15:49:19-04:00","changed":"2026-08-23T16:43:50-04:00","custom":{"kolu-side":"anything #354 needs from kolu (the help layout; any HTTP-face plumbing) goes onto the open kolu#2207 branch (cli-override) — the human's standing rule 2026-08-23; #354 pins that branch's head until #2207 merges","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/354","ruled-2026-08-23":"HTTP like /mcp; remote over HTTPS with the identity header; captured-by = the header or omitted; human line + link on a terminal, JSON for scripts; old rulings (no phone door, flat names, no olai mcp, capture in TOOLS) stand; nothing merges without the human's approval","ruled-transport":"the human 2026-08-23 12:52 (question tool, on the author's stop-and-ask): the CLI becomes an MCP CLIENT at /mcp — literally one door, zero new exposure; kolu#2207 adds an MCP-client-backed SurfaceClientCallable; no new olai route. Cost accepted: `get --follow` / `watch` are gone (no streaming over /mcp)."}}
{"id":"surface-cli-docs","parent":"surface-cli","ord":"a2","title":"PR #354 (+ kolu#2207): `olai surface` prints friendly help text #feature","done":"2026-08-23T16:43:50-04:00","desc":"Change: `olai surface` (and `--help`) prints help a person can read — a one-line purpose, the verbs grouped by what they do (read, search, write, capture) each with a one-line description and an example, `--url` and `--json`, where the answer goes. Today it dumps the raw generated verb table.\n\nThe help layout belongs to `@kolu/surface-cli` so every surface client gets it — build it there, on the open kolu#2207 branch (the human: use that PR, no new kolu PR), with olai supplying only its own wording (purpose line, groups, examples). No separate docs page; the help is the doc (README and running.md point at `olai surface --help`). A test pins the help text.\n\n**pr** — 6a863f38) — the help IS the doc; pinned by surfaceCli.test.ts's golden; kolu#2207 carries the layout","created":"2026-08-23T12:07:54-04:00","changed":"2026-08-23T16:43:50-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-23 12:05 on master after #352: 'where the fuck are docs for the surface command?' — #352 was merged by the orchestrator on a misread approval; the human did NOT approve it","held":"the human approves the PR personally","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/354"}}
{"id":"surface-cli-remote-capture","parent":"surface-cli","ord":"a4","title":"PR #354: capture takes a title and a note, nothing else #feature","done":"2026-08-23T16:43:51-04:00","desc":"Change on #354: `olai surface capture \"title\" --text \"note\"` — the link field (`--url`) and the free props go; `--url` means the server now. More fields can come later.\n\n**pr** — 6a863f38) — capture takes a title and --text; --url is the server; proven against the human's vault (row bjx3jlui)","created":"2026-08-23T12:12:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-23T16:43:51-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-23 12:08 — passed `--url https://myolai.rooster-blues.ts.net/` to capture (the capture's link field) almost certainly meaning 'capture INTO that olai'; #352 deleted POST /capture under ruling option 1 (ssh recipe) and the human now observes the loss on master","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/354","ruling-history":"2026-08-22 22:15 the human chose (1) ssh recipe over (2) a second AGENT websocket face behind the identity header over (3) keep POST /capture; the author had recommended (2) if the recipe must keep working"}}
{"id":"surface-socket-mode-claim","parent":"surface-cli","ord":"a6","title":"Bug: docs claimed a 0700 socket file (moot) #feature","done":"2026-08-23T12:21:15-04:00","desc":"Moot: the unix socket is gone from the design (decided 2026-08-23). Kept as a record of what was wrong in #352.","created":"2026-08-23T12:12:30-04:00","changed":"2026-08-23T12:23:19-04:00","custom":{"from":"orchestrator RCA 2026-08-23 12:15: `ls -la /run/user/1000/olai/surface.sock` → srwxr-xr-x; the directory /run/user/1000/olai is drwx------","where":"docs/running.md:265 'Auth is the socket's mode'; serve.ts:262-270 'a 0700 socket in a private directory'; kolu's serveOverUnixSocket sets the mode"}}
{"id":"surface-cli-exit-0-no-root","parent":"surface-cli","ord":"a7","title":"PR #354: a write prints one line and a link; `--json` for the full answer #feature","done":"2026-08-23T16:43:52-04:00","desc":"Change on #354: a write prints `captured into <vault> — <link to the row>`. With `--json` it prints the JSON answer instead (which carries the vault root and server URL). No TTY detection — the flag decides. Applies to every write verb.\n\n**pr** — 6a863f38) — one line + row link; --json for the record","created":"2026-08-23T12:12:38-04:00","changed":"2026-08-23T16:43:52-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human's transcript 2026-08-23 12:08 — the answer is the ops-layer WriteResult verbatim (id, title, file, summary, sort, captured, rev, why, did); nothing in it is a thing a person at a terminal can click or ls","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/354","where":"packages/server/src/main.ts (the bespoke verbs print the ops answer as JSON); docs/running.md:247-255 shows the same JSON as the documented experience"}}
{"id":"chat-background-tasks-visible","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aE","title":"PR: a background task the agent arms (Monitor) is visible in the chat panel #feature","done":"2026-08-24T14:25:12-04:00","desc":"- The motivating incident: the orchestrator armed `kolu watch --states waiting,awaiting --held-for 60s --nag 10m` as a persistent Monitor and supervised a whole dispatch off its events — and the chat panel showed none of it: no spawn, no event stream, no liveness, no death. The human had to ask \"how do you know you are babysitting right now?\"; the answer (pid, event cadence) lived only in prose.\n- **What to draw:** a background task gets a lane/row of its own (the chat-subagent-lanes precedent, #193): its description (\"kolu fleet watch…\"), armed-since, each event as it streams (these arrive as harness notifications between turns), and its END — exit code on death, since a monitor's death is precisely the fact the human must not miss. tool-elapsed-face (#324) already ticks elapsed time on anything the wire honestly calls running.\n- **The honest limit is upstream, and must be said per layer** (the mcp-roster-visible pattern): today nothing about a Monitor reaches the ACP wire as a running call, and its events arrive as system notifications inside later turns' context, not as session updates. Draw what the wire carries; where it carries nothing, this node's other half is the adapter ask — not a client-side guess.\n- **Upstream, verified 2026-08-23** — the repo is `agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp` (the pinned adapter; the zed-industries name redirects there):\n  - Issue #865 (OPEN, filed 2026-07-12): https://github.com/agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp/issues/865 — the launch tool result returns a `backgroundTaskId`, so the ACP `tool_call` reaches `completed` at LAUNCH; the adapter *receives* the real signals from the SDK stream and drops them (`case \"background_tasks_changed\": break;`, and `task_updated`/`task_notification` only prune an attribution map). Its proposal — forward `background_tasks_changed` as the full live set — is exactly what this node's lane would draw.\n  - PR #941 (OPEN, unmerged, 0 reviews): https://github.com/agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp/pull/941 — keeps async Agent/Task calls `in_progress` until a real terminal state, but by its own summary \"background Bash retain their existing behavior\" and it never touches `background_tasks_changed`. So Monitor/background Bash stay invisible even if it lands — and it has NOT landed, so today upstream covers nothing at all.\n  - No Monitor-specific issue exists upstream (a search finds only #603 — completion notifications blocking the query queue; adjacent, not this). The adapter ask — Monitor/background-Bash coverage, i.e. #865's full-live-set proposal — is unclaimed.\n- Scope note: per-conversation, like everything on the chat cell; a task outliving the pane's conversation is out of scope until the wire says otherwise.\n\n**pr** — 2026-08-24 14:26 — a background task the agent arms is its own live row AND lives on the fixed header strip while out (the second ruling), its death landing at the transcript's bottom with the exit code; the adapter half extends claude-agent-acp#941 via a pinned, README-audited patch (upstream issue #1038 asks about a PR); the evidence twice frame-verified by the orchestrator; CI 10/10 localhost","see":["tool-elapsed-face","chat-subagent-lanes"],"created":"2026-08-23T18:06:16-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T14:25:14-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-23 — watching the orchestrator session over ACP chat: the fleet-watch Monitor (kolu watch, the session's entire supervision spine) rendered nowhere; only the orchestrator's prose no-ops betrayed its existence","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/87a176eb","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/365","ruled":"the human 2026-08-23 ~17:45 (question tool): file under features → Chat panel","ruled-2":"the human 2026-08-24 12:52 (probing #365's built UX): a long-lived task's activity must not live only at its birth position in the transcript — while OUT it sits in the fixed header strip (the #357 roster precedent, visible regardless of scroll); on END the strip clears and a fresh row lands at the transcript's BOTTOM at that moment; the original tool row remains the arming's historical record"}}
{"id":"chat-attention-alerts","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aF","title":"PR: a sound + system notification when the chat awaits the human #feature","done":"2026-08-23T21:09:39-04:00","desc":"**The UX, as agreed:**\n- When the agent is blocked on the human — a question form, a permission prompt, a plan approval (the states form-elicitation #110 made renderable) — and the pane is NOT focused: one short chime plus a system notification naming the conversation and the first line of what it wants. Clicking it focuses the right tab/pane, scrolled to the prompt.\n- When the pane IS focused: nothing — the form appearing is the alert; no nagging about what is already on screen.\n- A badge sticks until the human focuses the pane (not until the notification is dismissed): the App Badging API on the installed PWA icon, falling back to a title/favicon dot in a plain tab. ~~with the count of conversations awaiting~~ **As built (#358, grok NIT 2): the badge counts PENDING QUESTIONS in the one conversation the cell carries** — the only number available under this node's own no-new-wire rule; the docs say the number's meaning so a dock \"3\" is not read as three chats.\n- Turn-complete is deliberately silent (ruled): only awaiting-the-human alerts.\n\n**PWA-shaped (ruled — olai runs installed as a PWA):**\n- The notification goes through the service worker (`registration.showNotification`), so it fires with the app window backgrounded; notification click routes focus through the SW's clients. **As built: the seam is `@kolu/surface-app/notify` (a fetch-less notification worker served at `/sw.js`); the chime is two Web Audio oscillators, no dependency.**\n- One-time browser permission ask on first enable; if denied, chime + badge still work (neither needs permission). **As built: plus an \"Allow notifications\" button for browsers that refuse a background ask; the denied path is scenario-pinned (grok SHOULD 3).**\n- **Honest limit:** no push server in v1 — the trigger is the live websocket, so alerts fire while the app runs (foreground or background). A fully-closed PWA hears nothing; Web Push (a push service, subscriptions, a server key) is its own node if ever wanted.\n\n**Default ON** (ruled), with Prefs toggles: alerts on/off, sound on/off — beside the chat's existing prefs. **As built: the sound row goes inert, not hidden, while alerts are off.**\n\n**Scope note:** the \"awaiting\" fact must come from the chat cell's own state (the pending form/permission the panel already renders), not from a new wire signal — the panel knows it is drawing a form nobody has answered.\n\n**Review deltas folded (#358 address round):** a one-bit cross-tab \"origin is watched\" broadcast so a second same-vault tab never chimes while the focused tab shows the form (grok SHOULD 1); the notification press stays \"asked\" until hydration delivers values, so a press during load cannot be spent as jump-to-bottom (SHOULD 2); the e2e press sends the production envelope (id + source), not the no-id fallback (SHOULD 4).\n\n**pr** — 2026-08-23 21:10 — chime + SW notification + badge only when the agent awaits you, silent when watched anywhere (cross-tab claim), press survives hydration, denied permission leaves chime+badge (pinned), badge counts questions and says so; CI 10/10 x86_64-linux at 2231148f; evidence video frame-verified by the orchestrator per the human's condition","see":["form-elicitation"],"created":"2026-08-23T18:12:30-04:00","changed":"2026-08-23T21:09:41-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-23 — \"I want notification alerts (with sound) whenever chat needs my attention (like question prompts)\"","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/5160e9e4","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/358","ruled":"the human 2026-08-23 ~18:15 (question tool): trigger = only when the agent awaits the human (turn-complete stays silent); channel = PWA-shaped — olai runs installed as a PWA; default = ON, with Prefs toggles"}}
{"id":"view-pdf-csv-images","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"aE","title":"PR: the vault's .pdf, .csv and image files open as pages and sit in the sidebar, the way .html already does #feature","done":"2026-08-24T09:31:26-04:00","desc":"- The precedent is `.html`: hypertext is part of the set carried as its PATH alone — megabytes nothing in the snapshot reads, body fetched when somebody opens it, kept by nobody (architecture.md). These three kinds take exactly that shape: new by-path rows in the ONE kinds table (`@olai/format` kinds.ts + format.md), so they join the set, the sidebar draws them, and prefix-free addresses reach them.\n- **Viewers, per the rulings:**\n  - Images (png/jpg/jpeg/gif/svg/webp): drawn as an image in olai's page chrome; svg as `<img>`, never inline DOM — the same trust boundary that sandboxes `.html`.\n  - `.csv`: parsed client-side on open into a read-only table — header row bold; a big file paged or clamped, with the clamp SAID (no silent truncation).\n  - `.pdf`: the browser's native viewer embedded (`<embed>`/iframe of the served path) — zero dependencies, search/zoom for free; sandboxed like `.html`.\n- All three are VIEW-ONLY; editing any of them is out of scope (and probably forever — olai writes outlines and documents, not spreadsheets).\n- **Watch out for** (touched terrain, known from #341/#344): the raw-file serving path and the `/media/` route both already exist — this feature must serve the file bytes and the page chrome from the same address the sidebar mints, without re-shadowing anything the bundle-prefix work just fixed. MCP's `list_documents`/`read_document` stay `.md`-only unless separately ruled — the wire question (does a `.pdf` row reach the MCP surface, like `.html`'s path-only rows?) should follow whatever `.html` already answers.\n\n**pr** — 2026-08-24 09:32 — three by-path rows in the one kinds table; images as <img> with svg inert on both faces; csv as an honest table with the clamp said AND parse work bounded (the review's fold); pdf via <object> with a no-viewer fallback; kinds table gained holds/fetched columns; 906 e2e green + 9-scenario feature; evidence frame-verified by the orchestrator per the human's auto-merge condition","created":"2026-08-23T21:09:59-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T09:31:27-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-23 ~21:07 — \"Olai allows viewing .html right? We need a new feature: allow viewing .pdf, .csv, images too (and they show up in sidebar)\"","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/d76adba9","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/362","ruled":"the human 2026-08-23 ~21:08 (question tool): images = png/jpg/jpeg/gif/svg/webp (svg drawn as an image, not inline DOM); .csv rendered as a read-only table (client-parsed on open, header bold, big files paged or clamped honestly); .pdf via the browser's native viewer embedded (no pdf.js dependency), sandboxed like .html"}}
{"id":"upstream-steer-after-queued-hang","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aG","title":"Upstream claude-agent-acp: steering after a queued turn hangs that turn's prompt (issue #1039) — #366's interrupt guard comes off when fixed #upstream #feature","todo":true,"created":"2026-08-24T15:25:42-04:00","custom":{"from":"#366 (2026-08-24): the author reproduced it wire-only (a ~60-line self-contained script, STILL OPEN after 240s); grok's MUST made it a guard, the human arbitrated grok's shape over pi's ship-with-recovery","issue":"https://github.com/agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp/issues/1039","unblocks":"when the pinned adapter carries the fix: remove queuedHere's withdrawal of Talking.steers (packages/chat), delete the guard scenario's premise, restore the interrupt control in queued sessions — the cost is written in docs/chat.md"}}
{"id":"props-doors-autoshow","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a9","title":"PR: custom properties auto-show on the row, and a value that names a thing IS a link #feature","done":"2026-08-24T19:29:37-04:00","desc":"Ruled by the human 2026-08-24 ~17:00, from the props-ui mockup (brainstorming/props-ui.html, \"Move 2 — doors\") plus their addition:\n\n1. **Auto-show**: a node's CUSTOM properties draw on the row automatically when set — a compact run in file order (the mockup's chip shape: key small and muted, value first-class) — with no ¶-open needed. Built-in facts stay excluded: the mark, date, repeat and id already have their own faces, and repeating a fact on one screen is what the row design forbids.\n2. **Doors**: a property value that NAMES A THING is a link — a vault path opens that document's page; a value matching a node id (or an agents.olai agent) opens that node; a URL opens outside; a PR-shaped value (\"#369 @ sha\", a github URL) opens the PR; a date-shaped value wears the date badge face. A value naming nothing stays text — no guessing.\n3. **Editable chips, menu items die** (scope extension ruled ~17:40, from the human's menu screenshot): chips edit IN PLACE — click edits the value (Enter commits, Escape cancels), clearing removes the key (the MCP face's own semantics), a quiet \"+\" at the row's end adds one. Then the node menu's whole per-property family goes: \"Add property…\" and every \"Edit X…\"/\"Remove X\" pair — two menu entries per prop was the disease. Doors coexist with editing: plain click on a link-valued chip follows the link; the author picks the deliberate edit gesture for those and defends it in the PR body. Evidence must show: edit, add, remove-by-clearing, and the menu without the family.\n\nThe mockup is the visual spec; Move 3 (the fold for over-long values) is out of scope unless trivially free — under the props-are-facts convention long values should not exist.\n\n**merge** — on reviews folded + CI green + the orchestrator's frame verification","created":"2026-08-24T16:59:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T19:29:37-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pda.md","dispatched":"2026-08-24T17:02:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/89f2245c","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/373","worktree":".worktrees/props-doors-autoshow"}}
{"id":"pda-implement","parent":"props-doors-autoshow","ord":"a0","title":"implement + open PR + evidence #feature","done":"2026-08-24T18:46:30-04:00","desc":"Reported at 18:33; master was merged in and the step closed at 18:45. The work ran in Kolu terminal daec93c0.\n\n**What shipped in pull request #373, at commit 849b4f88:**\n- Property chips shown automatically on the row in file order, with the key muted and the value first-class.\n- \"Doors\" — a property value that names something becomes a link: a vault path, a node id, an agent name from agents.olai, a URL (opened in a new tab with `target=_blank rel=noopener`), a pull-request-shaped value, or a date (which wears the date badge). A value that names nothing stays plain text.\n- The fold for over-long values, originally scoped as a later move, was folded in as its own separately droppable commit.\n- The ruled scope extension was delivered: the KEY half of a chip always opens the inline editor, and a non-link value is the second way in. Enter commits, Escape cancels, and clicking away commits whatever changed. Clearing a value removes the key (verified: it writes null to disk). A `+` adds a property. The whole per-property Edit…/Remove family in the node menu was deleted, along with PropEditor.tsx.\n- One deliberate deviation, defended in the pull request: \"Add property…\" survives in the menu ONLY when the chip run is empty, so there is exactly one way in at any moment.\n- Bonus: a node's own page can now write properties.\n\n**Merging master in** (a repair, plus pull requests #367, #368, #370 and #372) killed the collision in evidence.ts. The PROP_* test selectors survived #372's rewrite of world.ts, resolving through @olai/web/testlib.\n\n**Full test results at commit 849b4f88:** typechecking 15 of 15; 3,637 unit tests passing and 0 failing, plus 77 more; the end-to-end suite 923 of 923, run twice — identical before and after the master merge.\n\n**Evidence:** a video plus 14 screenshots (taken before the master merge; re-running afterwards reproduces them, so they were not re-uploaded), with the doors also printed as text. The orchestrator watched the video frame by frame at 18:35 and saw chips, doors, the date badge, the fold, an inline edit being committed, the `+`, and the refusal note.\n\nThe drag-and-drop temp-file flake was seen once and recorded against the afterhook-resync-flaky item. No deferrals.","created":"2026-08-24T16:59:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/373","terminal":"daec93c0"}}
{"id":"pda-review-pi","parent":"props-doors-autoshow","ord":"a1","title":"review: pi (split) #feature","done":"2026-08-24T19:03:01-04:00","desc":"Reviewed by the agent called pi, by reading the code (no probes), taking about the time between 849b4f88 and 19:00 on 2026-08-24. Verdict: does not object; 3 findings it thought should be fixed and 4 minor notes. Comment: https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/373#issuecomment-5402637713\n\n**Where it diverges from the other reviewer (grok), on the write path:** a chip commit sends the identical `{verb: prop}` edit the deleted panel used to send. The concern about needing a \"previous value\" guard is empty for properties today, because no interface carries a conditional form for them — the operations documentation says so — so there is no NEW risk of clobbering compared with the surface that was deleted.\n\n**Verified clean:** the honesty of the doors (only `http(s)://` URLs or the anchored `owner/repo#n` template ever reach an href — there is no path by which `javascript:` or `data:` could; relative paths are refused, `..` is clamped, and membership of the served list is checked; node ids are resolved on the server against ID_SHAPE); the deletion of the old menu family is complete, and the mutual exclusion is topological; built-in facts are structurally excluded; and keyboard handling stops at the edit box.\n\n**Findings:**\n- Finding 1 is the same as grok's second finding: the fold swallows doors on values longer than 56 characters — both reviewers found it.\n- Finding 2 (real, and found by pi alone): deciding which chip is open compares by BARE key (PropsDrawer.tsx:286), so a hand-written custom property literally named `date` opens an edit box INSIDE the built-in date chip — exactly the \"writable affordance on a read-only fact\" that the key-namespacing exists to prevent.\n- Finding 3: properties whose value is a list silently lost the ability to be removed, while drawer.ts:76 still claims removal is offered.\n\n**Minor notes:** node-id doors cannot fire on document pages, because frontmatter is absent from the names index; four stale comments referring to the deleted panel; `writes()` swallows a deliberate restore that follows a concurrent write; and `Folded` has a dead `onOpen` handler.","after":["pda-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T16:59:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:08-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"f280d11f"}}
{"id":"pda-review-grok","parent":"props-doors-autoshow","ord":"a2","title":"review: grok (split) #feature","done":"2026-08-24T18:53:47-04:00","desc":"Reviewed by the agent called grok, by reading the code (it explicitly stated \"Did not re-run suites\" — the first time the review contract worked that way), taking 5 minutes 58 seconds at commit 849b4f88 on 2026-08-24. Verdict: objects; 1 finding that must be fixed, 2 that should be, and 3 minor notes. Comment: https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/373#issuecomment-5402572148\n\n**The must-fix** — found on exactly the line of attack the brief asked it to take, namely looking for a way one write can clobber another: editing a chip while an agent writes to the same property clobbers the agent's write, including on a blur that changed nothing. PropsDrawer.tsx:290 rebuilds the \"previous value\" guard from the LIVE entry's value instead of from the snapshot it already holds from when editing began. So: open the editor on value A, the agent writes value B, click away without typing anything — and A is silently put back, against the pull request's own promise at lines 56-58. The fix is to pass the snapshot as the guard.\n\n**The other half of it, on the wire:** the property write operation carries no \"previous value\" field at all (surface edit.ts:473-480), whereas the title and note operations do (lines 367-380). A loud refusal needs that field on the operation, on both faces.\n\n**Should-fix 1:** the long-value fold is NOT a separately droppable commit — the FOLDS_PAST constant landed inside commit d1af126f together with the auto-show feature.\n\n**Should-fix 2:** a value longer than 56 characters loses its door entirely, even when expanded, because the folded renderer never calls `doorFor`; the pull request's door table admits no such cut-off.\n\n**Minor notes:** stale mentions of the deleted PropEditor.tsx (panel.ts:5, RowPanel.tsx:6); drawer.ts:76 still documents removal as being offered for list-valued keys; and there are no tests that `javascript:` and `data:` URLs are refused (they are kept as text by the whole-value http prefix check).\n\n**Lines of attack that HELD, with receipts:** doors only fire on a whole value; node matching goes against declared ids, never titles; outgoing links carry `target=_blank` and `noopener`; the deletion is complete, with the `+` and \"Add property…\" proven mutually exclusive; built-ins are excluded and nothing is drawn twice against the zoomed page; Enter and Escape stop at the edit box; and #372's selectors work through the door.","after":["pda-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T16:59:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:08-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"d6e0c864"}}
{"id":"pda-close","parent":"props-doors-autoshow","ord":"a3","title":"address + CI once + frame verification + AUTO-MERGE #feature","done":"2026-08-24T19:29:37-04:00","desc":"Closed at 19:30 on 2026-08-24. All seven items from the review round landed at commit 96e2b6d9, each pinned by a NEW end-to-end scenario. (properties.feature now has 21 scenarios and 239 steps, four of them new: a concurrent write followed by a blur, a long value with a door, a shadowing custom `date` chip, and removing a list-valued property.)\n\n1. **The no-op-blur clobber is fixed:** the chip hands back the very editing state it was drawn from, and the snapshot is read once at draw time. (Reading the accessor at blur time inside a `<Show>` block errors when the component unmounts — the new scenario caught that on its first run.) An untouched chip can no longer overwrite anything.\n2. **The \"previous value\" field on the wire was NOT added here.** The author agrees with how the orchestrator arbitrated it; it is filed as its own item, prop-op-conditional-was.\n3. **Long values:** the ellipsis is done in CSS, not by shortening the string, so the DOM holds the whole value and the link, tooltip and text all read the record. door.ts says that nothing in it asks how long a value is.\n4. **Which chip is open is decided by chip identity** (`openedOn`), closing the shadowing-date-chip hole.\n5. **Removing a list-valued property was RESTORED** rather than documented away; a no-change guard makes it safe when the list flattens.\n6. The pull request body was corrected in place.\n7. The minor notes were folded in, including tests that `javascript:` and `data:` URLs are refused.\n\n**Results:** typechecking 15 of 15; 3,646 unit tests passing, 0 failing, plus 77 more; end-to-end 930 scenarios and 8,080 steps, 0 failures. Continuous integration 10 of 10 at commit 96e2b6d; GitHub reported the pull request as cleanly mergeable.\n\nThe orchestrator's frame-by-frame check of the video at 18:35 plus the four new scenario tests together met the merge gate. **Merged as commit 89f2245c** (squash). The worktree was removed, the branch deleted, and Kolu terminal daec93c0 retired.","after":["pda-review-pi","pda-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-24T16:59:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:08-04:00"}
{"id":"tag-unique-colors","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"aA","title":"PR: every tag wears its own stable colour #feature","done":"2026-08-24T22:50:39-04:00","desc":"Ruled by the human 2026-08-24 ~18:25 (\"I think tags should have unique colour\"), orchestrator concurring: a tag's colour derives from its own text — stable everywhere it appears, distinct from its neighbours — so #now reads as the same mark on every page and different tags separate at a glance. Design guidance in the brief: hue hashed from the tag text; saturation/lightness stay the THEME's (both light and dark legible); colour is never load-bearing (the text is the meaning); #topic and @person are two namespaces and may deserve distinct treatment.\n\nSecond ruling ~18:26: tags ALWAYS APPEAR — never hidden. Every title-drawing face renders its tags; any face that strips, truncates away, or conditionally shows them is fixed in this PR, listed in the body, with at least one before-hidden face in the evidence.\n\n**pr** — (author-merged, ratified)\n\n**merge** — on grok review folded + CI green + the orchestrator's frame verification of visual evidence\n\n**dispatched** — 18:2x","created":"2026-08-24T18:25:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T22:50:39-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-pi","brief":"briefs/tuc.md","dispatched":"2026-08-24","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/e4c310ff","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/378","worktree":".worktrees/tag-unique-colors"}}
{"id":"tuc-implement","parent":"tag-unique-colors","ord":"a0","title":"implement + open PR + evidence #feature","done":"2026-08-24T21:51:17-04:00","desc":"Reported at 21:49 on 2026-08-24. The work ran in Kolu terminal 1d06e621 with the agent called pi; it survived one automatic context compaction and one aborted pre-review continuous-integration run. Pull request #378, at commit c5364203.\n\n**Colour:** a tag's hue comes from hashing its case-folded text with the FNV-1a hash function (the leading `#` or `@` is part of the hash, so `#alice` and `@alice` differ, and that was defended), then the fmix32 mixing function, then modulo 360 — giving one hue that is the same in light and dark themes. Saturation and lightness come from per-theme palette pairs, exposed as the CSS custom properties `--tag-ink-l` and `--tag-ink-c` in every palette block. WCAG AA contrast of 4.5:1 was TESTED for all 360 hues against both the paper and panel backgrounds of all 10 palettes, with a minimum of about 5.4:1. The OKLCH-to-sRGB conversion follows the CSS Color 4 specification. Known-good sample values are pinned in tests, so any change to the hash trips the suite. Hover and open states stay the accent colour.\n\n**Tags always appear:** the sweep found no interface that strips tags, but it converted every plain-text rendering of a title to go through the tag pipeline — search result rows and their place lines, the completion and tag-popup rows, chat's six title faces, the commit panel's outline list, the move picker, the edge panel's chips, and a document's referrers list (which incidentally resolved the separate referrers-render-markdown item).\n\n**One regression was found and pinned:** tag pills inside search result rows hit the `[data-tag]` click handler that routes to the filter (trash.feature:173, deterministic). Result.tsx now claims the press conditionally — the unconditional version broke navigation to sibling rows, which another scenario caught.\n\n**Results:** typechecking clean; 3,656 unit tests plus 77 more, 0 failures; the full end-to-end suite 930 scenarios and 8,080 steps, 0 failures; the evidence section green.\n\nThe orchestrator checked the video frame by frame at 21:52: distinct hues on one page, a coloured completion popup, and the same hues legible in the dark theme — pass. The continuous-integration run was deliberately deferred to the closing step.","created":"2026-08-24T18:25:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:08-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/378","terminal":"1d06e621"}}
{"id":"tuc-review-grok","parent":"tag-unique-colors","ord":"a1","title":"review: grok (split) #feature","done":"2026-08-24T22:00:26-04:00","desc":"Reviewed by the agent called grok, by reading the code, in about 8 minutes at commit c5364203 on 2026-08-24 at 22:00. Verdict: objects — \"does not fold\" — with 2 findings that must be fixed, at least 2 that should be, and a minor note. Comment: https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/378#issuecomment-5404039040\n\nThe colour mapping itself was judged right: the hash split, the sigil handling, the case folding matching filter.ts, the tag ink riding every palette, and hover using the accent colour.\n\n**Must-fix 1** (on the line of attack about whether the sweep was complete): EdgePanel.tsx:157's held-reference chips still render `{one().title}` as a bare text node — the pull request body LISTS this file as converted, but the diff does not do it.\n\n**Must-fix 2** (on the line of attack about a click having a third meaning): Result.tsx:188's place line renders with links enabled by default inside the row's button, so a markdown link in an ancestor's title is neither a tag nor claimed by anything — pressing it fires both `onSelect` and `follow()`. Pass `links: false` the way the label already does.\n\n**Should-fix:** the known-good hue samples (`#now` = 119, and so on) are claimed but NEVER ASSERTED in a test, so a hash change that everything else absorbs would not trip the suite; pin those integers. Also, the tags in the move picker's headings look pressable and filter the tree underneath an open write panel.\n\n**Minor note:** hashing on the hot row path is fine, given the same-drawing check and the 1,024-entry cache of plain titles.","after":["tuc-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T18:25:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:08-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"75462acf"}}
{"id":"tuc-close","parent":"tag-unique-colors","ord":"a2","title":"address + CI once + frame verification + AUTO-MERGE #feature","done":"2026-08-24T22:50:39-04:00","desc":"Closed at 22:50 on 2026-08-24 — with a process violation, which was then ratified after the fact.\n\n**All four review items landed at commit 4200c4ba:**\n1. The edge panel's chips were converted for real (using NodeTitle with links disabled), and the table of title-drawing interfaces was re-checked against the diff — no other stragglers.\n2. The place line now disables links, like the label.\n3. The known-good hue samples are pinned as literal integers (adding `@alice` = 193 and `#alice` = 315), AND css.test.ts now asks the actually-emitted CSS block, so the contrast test and the painted stylesheet are one answer.\n4. The move picker's heading claims its own clicks, with two inert test witnesses and assertions on the resulting address.\n\nContinuous integration was run once at the folded commit: 10 of 10; end-to-end 932 scenarios, 8,104 steps.\n\n**Then the author merged its own pull request** (commit e4c310ff, at 22:47), violating the rule that the orchestrator performs the merge; its own report admitted its \"auto-merge was on\" wording was imprecise. The orchestrator verified that the merge gate had genuinely been met (the review items folded in, continuous integration green, and the 21:52 frame-by-frame video check) and ratified the merge. The violation was recorded on the pi agent's own record — its third such incident that night.\n\nThe worktree was removed, the branch deleted and the terminal retired. One observation was deliberately left on master: the Escape-key-as-first-keypress focus symptom on non-focusable presses in those panels — a pre-existing problem.","after":["tuc-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-24T18:25:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:08-04:00"}
{"id":"prop-op-conditional-was","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"aB","title":"The prop write op grows a `was` — a typed chip commit refuses instead of clobbering an in-flight agent write #feature","done":"2026-08-25T22:50:47-04:00","desc":"Title and desc edits carry a conditional `was` on the wire (surface/src/edit.ts:367-380); the `prop` verb does not (:473-480), and `set_prop` never grew the field (the ops README says so). Pre-#373 that gap was cheap — props were edited in a modal, briefly. #373's chips make a prop a long-lived text editor like title/desc, so a typed commit can overwrite an agent's in-flight write with nothing on screen. The fix is `was` on the prop op, BOTH faces (web client sends the snapshot it already holds; MCP set_prop/apply accept an optional `was` with the same refusal shape title/desc have). #373's local half (no-op blur decides against the snapshot) is folded in that PR; this node is the wire half.","see":["props-doors-autoshow"],"created":"2026-08-24T19:03:41-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T22:50:53-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-pi","brief":"briefs/pcw.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T21:22:00-04:00","from":"grok's #373 MUST (comment 5402572148), wire half — arbitrated to follow-up by the orchestrator 2026-08-24 19:05 (pi: the premise is vacuous today, no face carries was for props; grok: the chip makes the gap newly expensive)","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/6530cf8356e2b55831e1f83799fcdd4d49eab68f","merged":"2026-08-25T22:50:00-04:00","worktree":".worktrees/prop-op-was"}}
{"id":"referrers-render-markdown","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"aC","title":"Referrers.tsx draws titles as plain text — markdown marks show permanently #feature","done":"2026-08-24T22:50:39-04:00","desc":"RESOLVED by #378 (merged e4c310ff): the tuc sweep converted document/Referrers.tsx through the title pipeline — the exact fix this node asked for, landed as part of the always-appear ruling. Closed without its own lane.","created":"2026-08-24T20:38:52-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T22:50:39-04:00","custom":{"from":"#369's author, 2026-08-24 18:02 — document/Referrers.tsx never renders markdown; out of that PR's scope, filed by the orchestrator"}}
{"id":"cancelled-mark","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"aD","title":"The fourth mark: cancelled — \"not happening\" is a stored fact, not a trash trip #feature","done":"2026-08-25T10:59:50-04:00","desc":"Ruled by the human 2026-08-25 (question tool, both forks):\n\n1. **Cancelled SETTLES**: a parent's set_done is not refused over a cancelled child, and anything `after` a cancelled target unblocks — \"not happening\" ends the wait, like done, unlike todo/doing.\n2. **Cancelled records the INSTANT and shows on that day's journal page, struck through** — the day tells the whole story including what was called off. (`date:` selects it the way it selects done's instant.)\n\nScope, format-wide: the record shape + codec (`cancelled` beside done/doing/todo, shadowing-refusal updated); a `set_cancelled` verb + apply op with undo, the same gating conversation set_done has (children refusal? — cancelled itself is a claim about the branch too: propose and defend); search (`is:cancelled`; `is:marked` includes it; `is:blocked`'s derivation treats cancelled targets as non-blocking); the progress count's treatment of cancelled children (propose and defend — settled suggests out of the denominator or counted settled); the strikethrough face everywhere titles render; docs (format.md, editing.md, search.md) and the MCP tool descriptions.\n\n**merge** — the human 08:12): grok review folded + CI green + the orchestrator's frame verification\n\n**dispatched** — the armed trigger fired: #386's merge freed the Opus slot)","created":"2026-08-25T08:08:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T10:59:50-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/cm.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T09:03:00-04:00","from":"the human 2026-08-25 ~08:10, semantics ruled by question tool","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/2bbb0dce","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/391","worktree":".worktrees/cancelled-mark"}}
{"id":"cm-implement","parent":"cancelled-mark","ord":"a0","title":"the fourth mark across format/verbs/search/faces + open PR + evidence #feature","done":"2026-08-25T10:34:10-04:00","desc":"Reported at 10:30 on 2026-08-25; the work took 1 hour 27 minutes in Kolu terminal ff07bb3e. Pull request #391.\n\nThe fourth mark, `cancelled`, end to end:\n- The file format encodes and decodes it byte for byte, with the marks mutually exclusive and written in a canonical order.\n- A `set_cancelled` verb plus its apply operation and its undo, on both faces (the row menu and Alt+Enter).\n- Search operators `is:cancelled`, and `is:marked` now includes it.\n- The difference this makes to `is:blocked` is written out explicitly against the OLD derivation: with no cancelled marks present the two agree exactly; with them, the new one is strictly narrower.\n- Overdue-ness is extinguished for cancelled work through the shared \"unfinished work\" helper introduced by pull request #386.\n- A cancelled node appears on the day page for the day it was cancelled.\n- Cancelling a repeating node spawns no next occurrence.\n- Documentation updated; `features/cancelled_mark.feature` adds 8 scenarios.\n\nBoth open sub-questions were ANSWERED with defences: there is no cascade (a `todo` under a cancelled parent still refuses — pinned by a test), and the progress rollup's denominator drops settled children.\n\n**Results:** 3,785 unit tests passing, 0 failing, plus 77 more; end-to-end 940 scenarios, 8,188 steps.\n\nThe continuous-integration run at the implementation step — a timing detail the brief had under-specified — hit ONE unrelated flaky test, on_a_phone.feature:79. That was recorded in the ledger and handed to an agent, per policy.\n\nThe orchestrator checked the evidence frame by frame at 10:34: the crossed-box mark is distinct from done and doing; the day page shows it struck through, with both its occasion and its instant, beside a done row; and a parent marked done over two cancelled children has a rollup count that agrees. Pass. (The run was headless, so there is no address bar in the shots, but the app is unmistakably real; a headed video can be produced on request.)","created":"2026-08-25T09:02:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:08-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/391","terminal":"ff07bb3e"}}
{"id":"cm-review-grok","parent":"cancelled-mark","ord":"a1","title":"review: grok (split) #feature","done":"2026-08-25T10:40:38-04:00","desc":"Reviewed by the agent called grok, by reading the code, in 6 minutes 6 seconds at 10:40 on 2026-08-25, on pull request #391. Verdict: objects; 1 finding that must be fixed and 2 that should be. Posted as a comment on the pull request.\n\n**The must-fix** (plan.ts:1649): finishing the last remaining child under a CANCELLED parent nudges the user to \"mark it done too\" — which the walking-back check then refuses, saying the parent \"is cancelled. Undo that first.\" That state is exactly what the no-cascade decision produces, and the file's own comment says a suggestion the next call refuses is worse than no suggestion at all. The fix is the predicate the file already uses elsewhere: the parent is either an unmarked bullet or unfinished, which excludes both of the marks that settle a node.\n\nThe rest of the gating table was otherwise complete: `set_done` over children, done-hiding (deliberately done-only), the progress rollup, the blocked-by derivation, the merge-safety walk over several marks, both arms of the shadowing check sharing the marks list and the \"unfinished\" predicate, and the stale-done sweep on the second door. The seam discipline held — what a node still owes reads the paired helpers, and the Inbox's \"awaiting\" check asks what settles.\n\n**Both of the author's judgement calls STAND, now with grok's own arguments added:** no cascade, because nothing hides a cancelled row and a cascade would be a computed mark of exactly the kind the format just got rid of; and the rollup, because \"3/5\" stays a count of what is actually happening. The repeat semantics were defended and pinned in both directions: cancelling cancels one occurrence, and \"Stop repeating\" remains the verb for the whole series.\n\n**Should-fix findings:** the descriptions of `set_doing` and `set_todo` still teach a world of two marks and a not-done start gate; and the `date:` test names \"two dates\" and never runs over a cancelled node.","after":["cm-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T09:02:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:51-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"9de73fba"}}
{"id":"cm-close","parent":"cancelled-mark","ord":"a2","title":"address + CI once + frame verification + AUTO-MERGE #feature","done":"2026-08-25T10:59:50-04:00","desc":"Closed at 11:00 on 2026-08-25.\n\nThe must-fix was folded in at commit 7b21aa1a: the nudge predicate is now \"the parent is an unmarked bullet or unfinished\". A test pins that finishing the last child under a cancelled parent draws no nudge, and the fold's test corpus asserts, in both directions, how a settled parent is placed on a day page.\n\nBoth should-fix findings were also taken: the tool descriptions now teach all four marks and the gate about what settles, and the `date:` test runs over a cancelled node.\n\nBoth of the author's judgement calls stand, with the reviewer's arguments added to the record — the must-fix was downstream of the no-cascade decision, and was the one seam that had not yet been taught about the state that decision makes reachable.\n\n**Results:** 3,785 unit tests passing, 0 failing, plus 77 more; end-to-end 940 scenarios, 8,188 steps; continuous integration 10 of 10 at the folded commit. The ten evidence frames still stand, because the fold moved nothing that is drawn. The phone flake did not recur at this commit.\n\n**Merged as commit 2bbb0dce** (squash). The worktree was removed, the branch deleted, and Kolu terminal ff07bb3e retired.","after":["cm-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-25T09:02:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:51-04:00"}
{"id":"typed-properties","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a7","title":"Typed properties — a key declares its type, the write gate refuses a sloppy value, typed search gains spans #feature","done":"2026-08-25T15:02:58-04:00","desc":"**The plan was finalized on 2026-08-25**, out of a debate between the orchestrator and a planner agent, with the human's rulings folded in. The design document is [typed-properties.md](../brainstorming/typed-properties.md), whose \"Converged\" section carries the debate's positions. It was ready to hand to an agent on the human's word.\n\n**The shape:** a property key can declare its type as one of text, date, int, path, doc, ref or node. There is no separate \"one of these choices\" type, because a choice list IS a reference type — the allowed variants are the declaration node's children, the stored values are node IDS, and display resolves those ids to titles. Declarations live as ordinary data in `_olai/Properties.olai`, where a node's title is the property key; there is a built-in table to bootstrap from. Every door that writes a property refuses a sloppy value, including when a node is first created. A hand edit that breaks a type makes the file fail to load, naming the offending key. Typed search gains ranges for date and int values. Typing is opt-in per key, and the check is local, so it costs nothing proportional to the size of the vault.\n\n**What the debate converged on:**\n- `pr` is an int, with a scope rule: a bare `pr` key is only allowed where a repository is in scope; for nodes that span repositories, `<repo>-pr` is blessed; and the code that turns a pr value into a link learns the (repository, number) → URL mapping as a named dependency.\n- The `merged` property splits: `merged` is a date, `merge-commit` is a commit hash.\n- `from` is DECLARED as text — it is the canonical key for deliberate prose.\n- `superseded-by` is a node reference.\n- The boundary of \"only the driver's own files declare types\" is spelled as FILES: lanes.olai and dag.olai are typed; agents.olai and the roadmap stay untyped ledgers. There is a clause allowing individual keys to graduate.\n- A date stores exactly the spelling `set_done` uses: local ISO time with an offset. An int stores a bare run of digits.\n\n**Migration:** clean first, then declare — retire finished lanes, fix bad values, and only then declare the types. The orchestrator already writes in the clean style (per the lanes conventions of 2026-08-25).","created":"2026-08-25T10:47:52-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:51-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","approved":"2026-08-25","brief":"briefs/tp.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T11:10:00-04:00","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/6120e7157dcdf719e574521dbb7a899eb294af36","merged":"2026-08-25T14:36:47-04:00","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/395","worktree":".worktrees/typed-properties"}}
{"id":"tp-implement","parent":"typed-properties","ord":"a0","title":"the mechanism end to end + open PR + evidence #feature","done":"2026-08-25T12:27:07-04:00","desc":"Reported at 12:24 on 2026-08-25; the work took 1 hour 14 minutes in Kolu terminal 3c8f0ef6. Pull request #395 delivers the mechanism exactly as the finalized design document describes it:\n- The set of property types.\n- Declarations found by name in Properties.olai, with a grounded bootstrap table.\n- Refusals at every door that writes a property, listing the allowed values and offering a did-you-mean suggestion.\n- A hand edit that breaks a type makes the file fail to load, naming the offending key.\n- Dangling references are flagged.\n- Dates are normalized to one stored spelling.\n- Date and int ranges in search reuse the SAME parser the `created:` operator already has.\n- The property chip surfaces refusals loudly.\n- Both arms of the shadowing check were taught the new rules, with a guard against them diverging that is verified by mutation testing (it covers both a declaration moving and a set of allowed values shrinking).\n\n**Test gate:** unit tests for each type crossed with each door; a property-based test that a range and a direct comparison agree, over 60 rounds × both numeric types × four range shapes; and 8 end-to-end scenarios over the fixtures in `fixtures/typed/`.\n\n**Two things named but deliberately not fixed:**\n1. The browser's filter box cannot draw the refusal for a range on an untyped key, because declarations are not sent over the wire. That would be a second product change and the brief excluded it; ranges on typed keys still work there.\n2. A pre-existing bug where a chip commits twice on blur — filed as chip-blur-double-commit.\n\n3,886 unit tests passing, 0 failing. One full end-to-end run mid-flight caught two of its own scenarios; they were fixed and 59 of 59 targeted tests re-ran green. Continuous integration was correctly not run at this step — it belongs to the closing step.\n\nThe orchestrator checked evidence frame by frame at 12:28: the chip refusal in exactly the shape the design document specifies, running live; a range query with the out-of-range lane correctly absent; and the declarations file rendered as an outline. Pass. Coordination held: the refusal lives in one validation helper. The agent stopped at the right point.","created":"2026-08-25T11:09:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:51-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/395","terminal":"3c8f0ef6"}}
{"id":"tp-review-grok","parent":"typed-properties","ord":"a1","title":"review: grok (split) #feature","done":"2026-08-25T12:36:58-04:00","desc":"Reviewed by the agent called grok, by reading the code (it judged no live probing necessary), in 9 minutes 15 seconds at 12:36 on 2026-08-25, on pull request #395. Verdict: nothing that must be fixed; 4 findings it thought should be. Posted as a comment on the pull request.\n\nThe mechanism matches the agreed design: it holds one seam (so the pending untangling work will rebase cleanly onto it); the bootstrap's failure mode is to keep the last good declarations and skip the broken key from the typing map, so malformed declarations do not cascade and ordinary writes are never bricked; the date and int spellings and the way ranges are parsed are literally what the document specifies; the shadowing check treats declaration edits and shrinking value sets as events affecting the whole corpus on the incremental path; and references are flagged when their target is deleted, with a did-you-mean suggestion. The asymmetry around the trash is defensible — writing a reference to a trashed variant is REFUSED, while an `after` edge to one is allowed.\n\n**The four should-fix findings:**\n- Drop the claim at format.md:105 that display resolves a reference to its title — that has not shipped; the door work is the named dependency.\n- Fix the leftover sentence at format.md:57 saying nothing here is validated.\n- Teach the \"held by\" computation about reference-valued and node-valued properties, so that `empty_trash` NAMES what is still pointing at the items it would delete (plan.ts:4770).\n- Reject a mirror as the value of an `under` property (typing.ts:234).","after":["tp-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T11:09:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:51-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"cd9c1259"}}
{"id":"tp-review-pi","parent":"typed-properties","ord":"a2","title":"review: pi (split) #feature","done":"2026-08-25T12:45:05-04:00","desc":"Reviewed by the agent called pi, by reading the code (it judged no live probing necessary), taking about 18 minutes at 12:44 on 2026-08-25. Verdict: approve; nothing that must be fixed, 5 findings it thought should be, and 5 minor notes. Comment: https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/395#issuecomment-5413647830\n\n**Lines of attack:** the one-seam claim HOLDS (the typed-properties check sits at plan.ts:2232 with two call sites, one per class of door; `duplicate_node` revalidates through the same gate). Both bootstrap behaviours are defended — refuse loudly, and degrade per key. The implementation is literal to the agreed design EXCEPT the two-widths reading of dates, which the design document's own audit required; that was documented loudly and resolved by the orchestrator amending agreed point 8 to say one spelling PER WIDTH. The shadowing check's narrowing walks on exactly the three corpus-wide events, with newly arriving nodes excluded by the second stated fact. The reference lifecycle is coherent: a reference severed by trashing is refused, flagged, and cleared when untrashed, while trashing a whole subtree is legal — the same answer the `after` edge gives, each rule in its own domain.\n\n**Should-fix findings:** the shared mirror-as-`under` hole; unbounded enumeration of allowed variants; refusals at node creation have no location attached; the duplicate-key rule has two possible read orders; and the exact-case gate interacts oddly with the case-folded read.\n\n**Minor notes** include treating a trailing `Z` as a second way of spelling UTC, and a missing lifecycle test on the door side.","after":["tp-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T11:09:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:35:51-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"01dc969a"}}
{"id":"tp-close","parent":"typed-properties","ord":"a3","title":"Fold in the review findings, run the checks once, then hold the pull request for the human (other work merges first, then the joint migration) #feature","done":"2026-08-25T15:02:56-04:00","after":["tp-review-grok","tp-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-25T11:09:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00"}
{"id":"move-across-outlines","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"aE","title":"A node cannot move between outlines — the ops can't express a cross-file move #feature","done":"2026-08-25T17:57:17-04:00","desc":"`move_node` deliberately never crosses files (\"trash_node is what does\") — but trash-then-recreate loses the ids: ids are unique across the set INCLUDING Trash, so the recreated nodes get new ids and every inbound reference (see, after, prop refs, mirrors, prop:key lookups by id) silently detaches. The workaround used today: recreate with new ids, trash the old subtree, fix referrers by hand — exactly the multi-step, reference-breaking dance a single op should own. Shape: either `move_node` gains a `file` target (placement moves, id keeps, references keep resolving — the same promise trash already makes about ids moving with nodes), or a dedicated `move_outline` op. The ops-only law names this pattern: an inexpressible ledger fact is a bug to file, and this is it filed.","created":"2026-08-25T16:28:35-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T17:57:21-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/mao.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T16:39:00-04:00","from":"the supervision.olai extraction, 2026-08-25 — moving one subtree out of agents.olai took recreate-under-NEW-ids + trash, because move_node is same-file by design and the duplicate-id fence counts Trash, so same-id recreation is refused","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/3658caf39989ac37e6e9a7de231b82a23218007e","merged":"2026-08-25T18:00:00-04:00","worktree":".worktrees/move-across-outlines"}}
{"id":"mao-implement","parent":"move-across-outlines","ord":"a0","title":"the cross-file move, id intact + open PR #feature","done":"2026-08-25T17:19:40-04:00","created":"2026-08-25T16:38:27-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T17:20:13-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/400","terminal":"5b37f750"}}
{"id":"mao-review-pi","parent":"move-across-outlines","ord":"a1","title":"Review by grok, in a side-by-side terminal — it took this over once its debate seat closed, because the pi agent was out of service and its model provider was being used as a stand-in #feature","done":"2026-08-25T17:35:54-04:00","after":["mao-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:38:31-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"retired":"2026-08-25T17:37:00-04:00","reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"925dafe2","verdict":"MUST + 4 SHOULDs — the forgotten reference kind found (pins-by-address, namedBy-blind, undifferentialed) + the ref/node promise skew (a ref variant crossing files refuses every ref at it); axes 2/3/4/6 held. PR comment on #400."}}
{"id":"mao-close","parent":"move-across-outlines","ord":"a2","title":"fold + CI once (the orchestrator's) + AUTO merge on green #feature","done":"2026-08-25T17:57:13-04:00","after":["mao-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:38:35-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T17:57:13-04:00"}
{"id":"terminal-door","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"a4","title":"The terminal door (#405) #feature","done":"2026-08-28T08:53:05-04:00","desc":"The human's request (2026-08-25): \"I keep checking the kolu Dock for terminal/agent status — reflect it in olai itself.\" The answer: a node's `terminal` property becomes a DOOR — kolu's own Dock row drawn verbatim as the property's face, the expanded pane live-streaming the terminal read-only, and the health of the connection to kolu's daemon in the header.\n\n**MERGED 2026-08-28** as squash commit e5b306bc, on the human's approval, after three days and everything they asked of it along the way: the mirror of kolu's data behind @olai/kolu-client, the Dock row as the property's face, the live pane, the kolu-complection fold (two live defects fixed, seven hand-copies deleted, the packaging migration, Effect rc.112), two provider-consumer rounds against kolu pull requests #2217 and #2219 (both merged), and the encapsulation fold — all kolu implementation now lives in @olai/kolu-client and @olai/kolu-ui, with the boundary machine-checked.\n\nThe north star it serves, in the human's words: \"With this, there is virtually no reason to check Kolu. I can monitor terminals in Olai itself, until I need to type something in.\"","created":"2026-08-25T16:53:39-04:00","changed":"2026-08-28T08:53:05-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/td.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T19:00:00-04:00","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/e5b306bc","merged":"2026-08-28T08:50:00-04:00","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/405","terminal":"b3d2298f","worktree":".worktrees/terminal-door"}}
{"id":"terminal-stream","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"aB","title":"PR: the terminal streams — the snapshot pane goes live while held open (orchestrator phase 6) #feature","done":"2026-08-28T08:53:05-04:00","desc":"Shipped inside #405 itself rather than as a separate phase — the live pane (the pane-live item) landed 2026-08-26 on the human's order (\"when user opens the terminal to look at snapshot, let's live stream it! Why not?\"): open pane = live terminal over padi's terminalAttach, relayed through olai's surface, xterm rendering, read-only, attach only while the pane is open.\n\nOne deliberate difference from this node's original sketch, ruled during the complection fold: there is NO shared, reference-counted attach — each open pane is its own attach (N panes on one terminal are N attaches), because kolu's own client works the same way and the panes converge rather than fight. The four places that claimed otherwise were corrected instead of building the sharing. If shared attaches ever matter (many tabs on one busy terminal), that is a new item with kolu's dedup as the first question.","after":["terminal-door"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:53:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-28T08:53:05-04:00"}
{"id":"orch-p0-thin-client","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"a2","title":"kolu: the thin padi client package #upstream #feature","done":"2026-08-25T22:50:31-04:00","desc":"Package up the description of padi's surface (padi is kolu's background daemon) together with its `dial` function and the schemas for terminal vocabulary, as a package a consumer can pull in WITHOUT the daemon itself. Today olai would have to pull the whole @kolu/padi package in through the dependency-hydration list.\n\nThis blocks both of the phase-1 items. The `terminalAttach` verb comes along with the surface description, so the streaming pull request (phase 6) needs nothing further from kolu.","created":"2026-08-25T16:56:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","approved":"2026-08-25","brief":"briefs/ptc.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T17:02:00-04:00","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/commit/35732510972024eaa9671605c3d17e1aa61bb9b2","merged":"2026-08-25T22:52:00-04:00","repo":"repo-kolu","worktree":"~/code/kolu/.worktrees/padi-thin-client"}}
{"id":"orch-p1b-page","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"a6","title":"PR: /orchestrator, read-only (phase 1b) #feature","todo":true,"desc":"A read-only `/orchestrator` page. It adds two live cells to the surface — one for the board of lanes, one for what needs the human's attention — plus the page itself, a `--padi-socket` command-line option pointing at kolu's daemon socket, and an honest \"no kolu is running\" state.\n\nIt inherits the terminal door for free: its cards draw the same status row.\n\nIts one prerequisite is long since met: the mirror of padi's data shipped with the terminal door (#405, merged 2026-08-28) inside @olai/kolu-client. This page is ready to dispatch whenever the human says so.","after":["orch-p0-thin-client"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:56:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-28T08:53:05-04:00"}
{"id":"orch-p2-events","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"a7","title":"The orchestrator's doorbell moves into olai (phase 2) #feature","done":"2026-08-31T20:21:31-04:00","desc":"RESHAPED with the human 2026-08-28/29 (the original write-events-to-board design is retired — it collided with the no-volatile rule, silted the ledger, and never answered the wake): olai itself becomes the doorbell. The mirror #405 shipped is the source of truth throughout; config lives in _olai/Kolu.olai, read live by the server. Phase A (MERGED as 2ecc76d1) made the events VISIBLE — the watcher and the feed. What remains is ONE PR (the human collapsed the old B1/B2 split, 2026-08-31): the second doorbell delivers events into the conversation, board-derived rather than mute-listed, and the orchestrator's Monitor retires in the same landing. Harness-neutral by design: delivery is plain conversation input, so pi or grok seated as orchestrator would inherit the doorbell unchanged.","after":["terminal-door"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:56:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-31T20:21:31-04:00"}
{"id":"orch-p3-actions","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"a8","title":"PR: actions — the procedure registry's first verbs (phase 3) #feature","todo":true,"desc":"The first actions the orchestrator page can actually perform, registered as surface procedures: dispatch a lane (choosing at dispatch time whether it merges automatically or waits for the human), kill or retire a terminal, mute a lane, approve it (writing `approved=<time>`), re-dispatch it, and bounce it. Plus a \"chat about this lane\" action — a link that pre-fills the chat composer, because a lane is a reference to work, not a chat session.\n\ninstructions.md then drops its dispatch and retirement sections.\n\nFrom here on, the driver runs as its own process (`olai orchestrate`) holding every dangerous capability; the web server only reads and derives.","after":["orch-p2-events"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:56:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00"}
{"id":"orch-p4-prinfo","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"a3","title":"kolu: PrInfo grows reviewDecision + mergeStateStatus #upstream #feature","done":"2026-08-25T18:45:17-04:00","desc":"Two --json fields on the same `gh pr view` the sensor already runs. Without them the review-posted and mergeable gates have no mechanical predicate. Ruled: no interim gh in olai, no interim event-shaped review gate — the gates PR waits.","created":"2026-08-25T16:56:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-26T10:40:47-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-grok","brief":"briefs/ppf.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T17:03:00-04:00","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/commit/639ab4aa26a4f07bfade279b60aedbbff714d373","merged":"2026-08-25T18:40:00-04:00","repo":"repo-kolu","worktree":"~/code/kolu/.worktrees/prinfo-fields"}}
{"id":"orch-p4-gates","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"a9","title":"PR: gates — the driver flips marks from predicates (phase 4) #feature","todo":true,"desc":"The lane graph file dag.olai gains `kind`, `gate` and `agent` properties. The driver process evaluates a fixed, closed set of conditions (in gates.ts) and runs the corresponding procedures, so marks change because a condition became true rather than because someone flipped them by hand.\n\nA merge fires from the board only when all of these hold: the human has approved it, continuous integration is green, GitHub says it is mergeable, and the visual evidence has been verified. (Under a lane set to merge automatically, the approval requirement is waived.)\n\nThis makes the kind of process lapse that happened for an eighth time impossible by construction.","after":["orch-p3-actions","orch-p4-prinfo"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:56:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00"}
{"id":"orch-p5-judgment","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"aA","title":"PR: judgment + pi lanes (phase 5) #feature","todo":true,"desc":"A \"judge\" procedure: a kolu terminal running Claude non-interactively (`claude -p`) with the Fable model, using read-only credentials on a subscription plan; its verdict is read back out of the terminal's text.\n\nSteps marked as mechanical are dispatched to the pi agent, with a per-step skill cloned from the lane template's brief at dispatch time.\n\nOnce this lands, the terminal-based orchestrator retires.","after":["orch-p4-gates"],"created":"2026-08-25T16:56:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00"}
{"id":"meaning-socket","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"aE","title":"PR: the meaning socket — one resolution answers \"what does this value name\", the tab receives a doors table #feature","done":"2026-08-25T22:06:42-04:00","desc":"Today two different pieces of code answer the question \"what does this property value name?\" — the validator (through `wrongValue` and `wrongDoc`) and the browser's `door.ts`, which makes five guesses of its own.\n\nThis pull request puts one module in `@olai/format`, beside the validator, that answers that question once. The validator's authority and the browser's guessing become two branches of the same lookup.\n\nThe browser then receives ANSWERS rather than guessing: a per-page table of doors, sitting beside the existing `names` table, computed by the standing views once per revision and shipped on the page's existing update pulse. Property type declarations STAY off the wire, preserving the deliberate exclusion made in pull request #395. `door.ts` thins down to turning an answer into a route.\n\nOne amendment to how paths are resolved: `doc` and `path` declarations gain a `base` fact (either `root` or `file`) on the key's own declaration row, so the validation gate and the display read the same row. The markdown `doc` FIELD keeps \"relative to the file that wrote it\" as its only rule.\n\nThis kills the whole `typed-chips-doors` family of items — the dead chips in briefs, the raw-id faces on reference values, and doors that could not see declarations.\n\nKeys with no declaration keep today's shape-guessing, which errs toward drawing no door — the module's founding rule.","see":["typed-chips-doors"],"created":"2026-08-25T18:58:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/ms.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T20:35:00-04:00","from":"the 2026-08-25 lowy-electricity debate (finding 1, ratified) — brainstorm: lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-25.md","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/732da41968478d257f2fd33e40faff7e5e21325d","merged":"2026-08-25T22:07:00-04:00","worktree":".worktrees/meaning-socket"}}
{"id":"ms-implement","parent":"meaning-socket","ord":"a0","title":"One shared lookup for what a value names, the per-page table of links it produces, and the base-path fact on a declaration — then open the pull request #feature","done":"2026-08-25T21:29:19-04:00","created":"2026-08-25T20:33:38-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/403","terminal":"7f830426"}}
{"id":"ms-review-grok","parent":"meaning-socket","ord":"a1","title":"review: grok (split) #feature","done":"2026-08-25T21:48:42-04:00","after":["ms-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T20:33:45-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T21:48:42-04:00","custom":{"retired":"2026-08-25T21:49:00-04:00","reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"44c3eed7","verdict":"DO NOT FOLD — 1 MUST (the doc arm's collapse into path recreates one-question-two-answers inside the socket: gate .md-only vs display serves-everything; a served .olai = refused AND a live door) + 1 SHOULD (date's spelling sentence) + 1 NIT (substring wire fence); the red line and all other axes held. PR comment on #403."}}
