{"id":"node-agents-concurrent","parent":"chat-subjects","ord":"a1","title":"PR: many node agents run at once #feature","todo":true,"desc":"Phase 2 of node agents (brainstorming/node-agents.md). Olai runs ONE conversation at a time — `agent.ts` holds `let session: string | null`, and `chat.ts`'s header says \"The one conversation\". Everything above inherits it: an agent that is not the open one HAS NO PROCESS, so it cannot be working, cannot answer, and cannot act on a wake. That is why #461's roster honestly counts *questions waiting on you* rather than unread messages, and why it needed seven standings (adding unbound, waking, gone) where the design named four.\n\n**The ceiling is OURS, not the protocol's** — verified in the pinned adapter: it already holds many sessions at once (`this.sessions` keyed by id, `teardownSession(id)` tearing down exactly one, `acp-agent.js:3429`), and every prompt is addressed by `sessionId`. So the work is olai-side: the agent module holds a MAP of live sessions instead of one; the panel routes turns and deliveries BY NODE rather than by \"the\" conversation; the roster's live states become true for several rows at once; and session economics (lazy spawn, idle reaping, a cap) become real policy rather than a consequence of there only ever being one.\n\n**Why it comes before the rest:** a derived wake for an unfocused agent is only HELD, not acted on — so autonomous supervision (phase: derived wakes) and an agent that dispatches its own child work (phase: agency) both presume this. Without it a node agent is a nicer switcher. Not dispatched; the design round settles the session-economics policy first.","after":["node-agents-p1"],"created":"2026-09-01T19:52:53-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-09-01 ~19:50: \"the next phase here is to support concurrent agents\""}}
{"id":"odu-baked-in","parent":"orch-odu","ord":"a4","title":"PR: olai's build bakes odu in #feature","done":"2026-09-01T22:56:19-04:00","started":"2026-09-01T20:11:26-04:00","worked":9893,"desc":"The fix for the root-caused odu-MCP absence (the finding on the Inbox's retire item): a deployed olai has no `odu` on its PATH — this host runs odu only via `nix run` — and the probe's absence arm is silent by design, so #458's tools half can never fire and nothing says so. THE HUMAN'S RULING (2026-09-01): install-on-host rejected — binaries are baked into the Nix build; and silent failures are banned. Two halves in one PR: (1) the packaged server's PATH always carries an odu built from the existing npins pin (precedents: nix/acp-agent.nix's wrapper, nix/home/module.nix's own-binary-on-PATH), every face — nix run, packaged binary, just serve, the unit; (2) probe.ts's quiet arm goes — a missing odu draws the missing-row like every other failure, test seen red first. Docs (chat.md's odu sections, plugins/odu.md) change with it. Author pi on the human's naming (overriding the reviewer-only seat note), auto-merge on their word; pi-authored ⇒ both grok and claude-opus review.","created":"2026-09-01T20:11:18-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T22:56:19-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-pi","brief":"briefs/odu-baked-in.md","dispatched":"2026-09-01T20:20:00-04:00","kolu-terminal":"3994ea28","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/37770c2e","odu-worktree":".worktrees/odu-baked-in","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/464","tier":"ordinary","timings":"implement 31m · refactor 21m · reviews 9m/8m parallel · address ×3 ~1h24m (1 compaction, survived) · re-verdicts 11m · ci 7m · merge <1m — TOTAL ~2h37m (00:20→02:57 UTC)"}}
