{"id":"lane-dnc-review-a","parent":"doorbell-nag-count","ord":"a2","title":"review: the human, by hand #feature","done":"2026-09-03T16:33:54-04:00","worked":454,"desc":"**The human reviewed #489 themselves and ruled the step complete, 2026-09-03 ~16:38 ET: \"consider reviewer complete on this one (i manually did it)\".** Grok's split had been stopped before posting, so nothing of an agent's review is on the PR.\n\n**Their verdict: the move is coherent** — olai relays the kind, the since and the nag accounting rather than counting, the episode ledger drops reconnect re-leads, and a knob edit is the only re-ask. The dominant residual risk they name is the events-drawer fold, which grew the reminder suffix without a test.\n\n**One bug, four suggestions, no nits**, posted as inline comments:\n- BUG, events.ts:117 — the drawer is a primary face of the new accounting and its fold is untested. The existing nag case still constructs a field-less event and asserts a suffix-less sentence, so the wording the header and docs advertise is unpinned and can regress with no red test. Watch tests lock the wire field and doorbell tests lock the body clause; neither exercises this fold.\n- doorbell.ts:1060 — the drawer gates the account on the kind and the doorbell does not, so a transition carrying a nag field would get a reminder line in the conversation and none in the drawer. The two faces exist precisely so a count cannot disagree with itself.\n- watch.ts:279 — a comment says the row cache is never evicted; the code caps it. Leftover from an earlier draft.\n- docs.md:120 — the quoted doorbell sentences omit the terminal id the body actually names, so an operator matching the docs against a delivered message will not find it.\n- config.ts:179 — the bare-number gate tests the untrimmed value while the parser trims first, so `nag: \"10 \"` slips the gate and becomes a 10ms re-fire spin. Same hole on the other two knobs.","after":["lane-dnc-refactor"],"created":"2026-09-03T07:04:29-04:00","changed":"2026-09-03T16:34:40-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok"}}
