{"id":"e2e-scratch-sharing","parent":"infra-tests","ord":"aD","title":"63% of e2e scenarios spawn a private server — share one scratch corpus per feature where files are disjoint #performance","done":"2026-08-20T11:12:01-04:00","desc":"555 of 875 scenarios are @scratch: — each a corpus copy plus an olai process spawned and killed. Families like the_agent (82) and html_previews (32) write disjoint files and could share one scratch corpus per FEATURE instead of per scenario.\n- Worth more wall time than all 150 audit cuts combined; also where the flake census (underload.sh) says the port/lock failures come from.\n- Deliberately out of the e2e-earn-browser cut lane's scope — an infra change with its own risk profile.\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — #281 (Grok, merged 232c1f8b7), with the premise revised by its own census.** The mechanism: `@share-scratch` on a feature → one corpus copy + one server per feature PER WORKER; collisions OBSERVED by content-hashing the scratch tree after each scenario and failing loudly naming both scenarios and the file; `@own-scratch` opts a scenario out (refused without the feature tag); a shared scenario that restarts its server is refused (`restartGate`, pinned). Census after the cut: 59 features, 724 scenarios, 490 `@scratch`.\n- **What did not hold:** the_agent (76) is NOT file-disjoint — the agent writes the same outlines and conversation state lives in the server and XDG; the UI-edit families all mutate one outline; it_stays_live/backlinks read the original corpus that later scenarios mutate (a write-only observer cannot catch that). Converted: html_previews (19 share / 9 own) and documents (2 / 1) — 16 of 502 spawns saved; converted-file underload 3 runs / 0 drops.\n- What is left is filed: `e2e-scratch-restore-between` (restore files under a live server so overlapping writers could share) and `e2e-underload-full-census` (the full-scale flake census).","created":"2026-08-19T20:50:58-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:56:09-04:00","custom":{"from":"the e2e audit 2026-08-19"}}
{"id":"e2e-scratch-restore-between","parent":"infra-tests","ord":"aE","title":"e2e: restore files between scenarios #deferral #performance","done":"2026-08-20T18:40:24-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-20 at #281's merge (the dag gate: a deferral becomes a roadmap node first; opencode classified this one as real work left, not a finding).\n- #281 shares one scratch + one server per feature per worker only where scenarios' writes are DISJOINT, observed by content hash; a feature whose scenarios rewrite the same file keeps per-scenario copies.\n- Restoring the corpus between scenarios under the still-running server would let overlapping writers share as well — but the store is entitled not to notice a same-length rewrite (the evidence README already says so), so a restore needs a signal the server honours, not just bytes put back. That is the design question.\n- Payoff ceiling: the UI-edit families (keyboard_editing, undo, dragdrop_multiselect, palette_actions, split_and_merge, trash, edge_editing — ~150 scenarios) are the ones this would reach; chat features stay private regardless (server/XDG conversation state).","created":"2026-08-20T11:06:19-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:56:09-04:00","custom":{"from":"olai #281 deferral"}}
{"id":"test-style-purge","parent":"infra-tests","ord":"aF","title":"Tests stop asserting styling — the suites purged per the HACKING.md rule #performance","done":"2026-08-20T19:53:40-04:00","created":"2026-08-20T19:19:19-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:56:09-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-20, after the owed.test.ts font-semibold find; rule codified HACKING.md d40516b2"}}
{"id":"transcript-stream-quadratic","parent":"bug-server","ord":"aD","title":"Chat streaming is quadratic in bytes AND stop-and-wait (one frame per round trip) — a 15 kB answer costs more wire than the whole app #performance","done":"2026-08-22T12:48:48-04:00","desc":"**Symptom:** chat answers stream in slow, lumpy pulses from kolu-bot (India) to a laptop (Canada) over a DIRECT tailnet (~250ms RTT; DERP ruled out via `tailscale status`).\\n\\n**Measured two ways — ACP chunk counts on kolu-bot, and a full browser+websocket instrument over an artificial delayed TCP link (scratchpad: chatwire.ts, proxy.ts, stream-agent.ts). Two independent defects:**\\n\\n**1. Quadratic bytes (every agent, every link).** The chat rebuilds the whole accumulated string and upserts the FULL row per chunk (transcript.ts:428 `#grow` → runtime.ts:1067 upsert), and kolu's coalescer is a `queueMicrotask` latch (kolu server.ts:713) that merges nothing because ACP chunks arrive on separate I/O ticks — one frame per chunk, each carrying the entire answer so far. Frame k ≈ k·chunk + ~190B envelope. Amplification ≈ chunks/2:\\n- opencode on kolu-bot: 3.2 kB answer → 643 chunks (~5B each) → **1.04 MB on the wire, 323×.**\\n- synthetic 800-chunk answer (37.9 kB) → **15.0 MB, 406×.**\\n- real Claude adapter, 14.6 kB answer → 843 kB — **more wire than loading the ENTIRE app** (HTML+JS+fonts+icons = 777 kB the same session).\\n\\n**2. Stop-and-wait: exactly one frame per round trip (unexpected, bigger on a long link).** The transcript stream ACKs each frame with a hard-coded credit window of 1 (effect RpcServer/RpcClient `supportsAck`), and kolu builds streaming members with `Stream.fromAsyncIterable` (kolu server.ts:372,409) — the NON-coalescing Effect branch — so each round trip carries one delta, never the batch accumulated during it. Time = chunks × RTT, independent of bandwidth (capping the link to 5 Mbps cost 8%; it ran 93% idle). At RTT 200ms, a 400-chunk answer the agent finished in 10.6s took **82.6s to finish arriving — the panel 72s behind, painting at 5 chunks/s.** 800 chunks: 147s behind.\\n- **When it bites:** any agent streaming faster than ~5 chunks/s at 200ms RTT. The pinned Claude adapter batches (~1.4 chunks/s) so it stays hidden today — the long-haul Claude run showed ZERO lag. opencode and every token-level streamer land at 20–40 chunks/s and hit the wall immediately, which is exactly the human's report.\\n\\n**Fix, cheapest first (the measurement ranks them):**\\n1. Throttle transcript.say / chat.ts:434 so chunks in one tick collapse before publish — kolu's existing microtask coalescer then genuinely merges them. Fixes BOTH defects, no protocol change. Start here.\\n2. Give the row an append representation (a chunk delta carries only its new text) — fixes bytes to O(N), independent of (1).\\n3. Make the transcript member produce via the Effect/Queue shape instead of `Stream.fromAsyncIterable` so a round trip carries the whole accumulated batch — fixes the RTT stall; a kolu-side change.\\n- Browser appends and throttles the Markdown re-render to match.\\n\\n**Not this node:** kimi-k3 via LiteLLM from India was also slow at the model level (first token 7.3s) — provider latency, separate from the wire.\n\n**pr** — merged 2026-08-22 as d6f80e78 (fixes 1+2, pin to kolu 020f05af) + https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2202 — merged as 020f05af (fix 3). Measured: 1,118 kB/643 frames → 19.6 kB/82 loopback; 1,093 kB → 18.6 kB over a 200 ms relay. The panel-behind number was not reproduced by the lane's relay instrument (0.2 s behind before) — verify on the real Canada↔India link after deploy.\n\n**kolu-pr** — fix 3 = juspay/kolu#2202 (re-land of #2199 with a bounded Stream.buffer(512, suspend) + settle-based membership tests), MERGED 2026-08-22 12:36 as 020f05af with all ci:: contexts green on both platforms (history: #2199 merged 8b9820ed without odu — orchestrator error — found red, reverted via #2201 = 9a1137b8); olai's pin moves to 020f05af in #345","created":"2026-08-22T08:39:49-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:56:08-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-22 (slow streaming, Canada↔India direct tailnet); measured on kolu-bot","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/commit/8b9820ed","pr-url":["https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/345","https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2202"]}}
{"id":"psw2-server","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-19","ord":"a6","title":"Server: every open tab pays for every keystroke","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:55:59-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-streams-per-tab","parent":"psw2-server","ord":"a0","title":"Five page answers are rebuilt on every write — once per open tab #performance","done":"2026-08-25T10:05:44-04:00","desc":"The page, filter, calendar, overdue and move-picker answers are each wired as a polling stream, and the framework runs one full recompute loop per subscribed tab, fired by every write (runtime.ts lines 967-1020). The \"did anything change?\" comparison runs after the rebuild — it saves the network frame, never the work — and each read also spins up a fresh fiber and, even on the nothing-changed path, walks the whole answer structurally to say so. So three tabs on the same page mean three identical rebuilds per keystroke.\n\nFix shape: compute once per revision per distinct question and share the result across subscribers, and find a cheap \"could this have changed?\" test that runs before the rebuild rather than after.\n\nMerge: approved by the human at 07:57 — merge once the Grok agent's review is applied and CI is green. The gate is a harness proving each subscriber sees the same deltas, plus proof, by deliberately breaking it, that the pre-check is doing work.\n\nDispatched in wave 1 of the sweep campaign, on the human's word.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pst.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T07:58:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/09fbd710","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/387","worktree":".worktrees/streams-per-tab"}}
{"id":"perf-git-per-write","parent":"psw2-server","ord":"a1","title":"Git is consulted about every uncommitted file on every write — and the bill grows until you commit #performance","done":"2026-08-25T09:08:39-04:00","desc":"Every revision runs `git status`, and then, for every dirty outline, a `git show HEAD:<file>` subprocess plus a full parse of the old copy, in order to build the node-level diff (runtime.ts line 617, pending.ts lines 800-860). Under manual committing the dirty list only grows during a session, so typing literally gets slower the longer a commit is deferred. The code comment admits only to \"one git status on a clean directory\".\n\nFix shape: HEAD's copy of a file cannot change between commits, so cache the parsed old copy per commit and file; or compute the detailed diff only when the Commit user interface actually asks for it.\n\nMerge: approved by the human at 07:57 — merge once the pi agent's review is applied and CI is green. The gate is a scripted git session compared against the old code kept as a reference, plus a subprocess count proportional to the number of changed files rather than to all of them.\n\nDispatched in wave 1 of the sweep campaign.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pgw.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T07:58:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/74c71eab","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/385","worktree":".worktrees/git-per-write"}}
{"id":"psw2-model","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-19","ord":"a7","title":"The model: costs that grow with the vault's age","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:55:59-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-agenda-history-walk","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a0","title":"The overdue count walks every past day — and fully dresses every overdue item — to produce two numbers #performance","done":"2026-08-25T09:02:25-04:00","desc":"`behind()` visits every day before today and builds the complete entry for each overdue node — parent trail, blockers, progress — even though the subscriber only wants two counts, and it is recomputed on every revision (agenda.ts lines 348-399, ops query.ts line 588). The forward walk and the calendar likewise step linearly over every earlier day instead of jumping to the start (agenda.ts line 398, dates.ts line 92). A vault with years of journal days pays for all of them on every write.\n\nFix shape: maintain the two counts as an index the patcher keeps up to date, following the dated-index precedent, and start day walks with a sorted lookup instead of stepping.\n\nMerge: approved by the human at 07:57 — merge once the Grok agent's review is applied and CI is green. The gate is a test proving the counts equal the walk's answer, plus assertions covering the midnight and mark-done boundaries.\n\nDispatched in wave 1 of the sweep campaign.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pah.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T07:58:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/e914a6c2","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/386","worktree":".worktrees/agenda-history-walk"}}
{"id":"perf-key-resort","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a1","title":"Editing near a popular tag re-sorts that tag's whole vault-wide list #performance","done":"2026-08-25T10:22:06-04:00","desc":"When a patch touches a key, the patcher rebuilds that key's entire member list — a copy plus a re-sort — even though only a few members moved (patch.ts lines 380-388). Typing in any file that mentions #kitchen re-sorts every #kitchen record in the vault; the same goes for a busy day in the byDay index, or a large parent's children. The header comment's claim that work is \"bounded by the touched file\" covers the folds above this line, not this rebuild.\n\nFix shape: splice into the already-sorted list instead of copying and re-sorting it.\n\nMerge: once the pi agent's review is applied and CI is green. The gate is a test proving spliced results equal rebuilt ones, plus proof, by deliberately misplacing an element by one position, that the test catches it.\n\nDispatched as the fourth item in the sweep queue; the trigger was PR #385 merging and freeing the slot.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pkr.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T09:09:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/73a63a10","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/389","worktree":".worktrees/key-resort"}}
{"id":"perf-overlay-copies","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a2","title":"The patcher copies its carried bookkeeping even when a patch doesn't touch it #performance","done":"2026-08-25T11:15:54-04:00","desc":"Each layered index deep-copies its carried layer in its constructor — three copies — and then throws them away untouched if the patch wrote nothing to that index (overlay.ts lines 280-333). The layer can grow to half the corpus before it is flattened, so a long editing session can pay up to three copies of half the corpus, times six indexes, per keystroke, for nothing.\n\nFix shape: copy lazily, on the first actual write to the layer.\n\nMerge: once the pi agent's review is applied and CI is green. The gate is the aliasing assertion, plus every existing differential test staying green without being modified.\n\nDispatched as the sixth item in the sweep queue, in the slot freed by PR #389 merging.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/poc.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T10:23:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/bc886435","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/392","worktree":".worktrees/overlay-copies"}}
{"id":"perf-doc-backlinks-index","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a3","title":"A document's page re-scans every link in every file, on every write #performance","done":"2026-08-25T11:37:38-04:00","desc":"\"What links to this document?\" has no reverse index — `referrersTo` loops over every face in the directory and tests every link (backlinks.ts lines 242-275), on every revision, for every tab sitting on any .md or .html page. The node-to-node direction next door is indexed; this is the one direction that is not, and the comment admits it.\n\nFix shape: one more maintained index, from links to documents, kept up to date by the patcher like the others.\n\nMerge: once the Grok agent's review is applied and CI is green. The gate is a test proving the index equals the scan, plus proof by deliberate breakage that the index's maintenance is really tested.\n\nDispatched as the fifth item in the sweep queue, in the slot freed by PR #387 merging.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pdb.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T10:06:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/52ba0f81","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/393","worktree":".worktrees/doc-backlinks-index"}}
{"id":"perf-filename-conventions","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a5","title":"Pins and the inbox re-derive their by-filename answers on every write #performance","done":"2026-08-25T16:23:28-04:00","desc":"Every revision re-allocates the full file list, lower-cases every base name and re-sorts, for answers that only change when a file is added, removed or renamed (shelf.ts line 192, inbox.ts line 99, runtime.ts lines 671-716). Comparing the resulting value only suppresses the network frame — the recomputation work is still paid.\n\nFix shape: recompute only when the set of file paths has actually changed.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pfc.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T15:05:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/9ef700e5a680593acca2fd4927db266c602488fb","merged":"2026-08-25T16:28:00-04:00","worktree":".worktrees/filename-conventions"}}
{"id":"psw2-ops","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-19","ord":"aA","title":"Ops: tool calls that touch everything","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-09-06T15:55:59-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-capture-paths","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a0","title":"capture builds every node in the vault just to learn the list of filenames #performance","done":"2026-08-25T13:25:59-04:00","desc":"Working out where a capture should land calls the full outlines listing, which materializes and filters every node in the directory, only for the file paths to be kept (ops query.ts lines 967-1024, tools.ts line 310). It is paid on every capture, and sometimes twice, because of the retry wrapper.\n\nFix shape: ask a paths-only question; the listing keeps its counts for list_outlines, which genuinely needs them.\n\nDispatched at 04:00.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pob.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T11:18:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/afc093d8","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/396","worktree":".worktrees/ops-walks"}}
{"id":"perf-batch-assemble","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a1","title":"One set of answers per batch, one planner per operation — plan.ts untangles and its per-operation whole-vault cost goes with it #performance","done":"2026-08-25T13:25:59-04:00","desc":"One change with two faces: the performance fix and the structural refactor are the same diff, which is the sign that the seam is real. Ruled with the human, 2026-08-25.\n\nThe performance face, which was the original finding: the batch fold rebuilds the full document set — a fresh map and a full path sort — once per operation, so a 100-operation batch pays for it 100 times (following.ts line 135, set.ts lines 152 and 304; the file's own header admits it). Beside that, every write naming a file does a linear search over all served paths (refusals.ts line 207, via plan.ts line 440 and similar), and the broken-files map is rebuilt at roughly 8 call sites per single write.\n\nThe structural face: plan.ts is 4,646 lines, about 2,500 of them code — the largest file in the vault — and it fails the test of doing one thing, not because of its length but because it interleaves three separate concerns: deciding what an operation means, asking the set the same questions over and over (those 8 call sites), and sequencing the batch. The file size is the shadow; the interleaving is the smell. filter.ts and derive.ts pass the same test — big but single-purpose — so they stay.\n\nThe one diff, three moves:\n1. A `PlanningContext` value — the assembled set, the paths as a Set, the broken-files map, and the homes — computed once per batch and passed in. The 8 repeat questions die, path lookup becomes a map hit, and the per-operation cost that grows with the number of files becomes a per-batch cost.\n2. Each operation's planner becomes an independent pure function of `(context, operation) → plan`, dispatched through a map — the shape the tools layer already has one level up. Whether plan.ts is then one file or eight is an editorial choice, not architecture.\n3. Plans stay data — they already are, refusals and writes being values — which is what makes these moves behaviour-preserving.\n\nNot the goal: splitting files for the sake of line counts. After the untangling, how the code is filed can follow taste.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"bundled-in":"perf-capture-paths"}}
{"id":"perf-homes-files","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a2","title":"Every fold click rebuilds two all-files structures — under a comment claiming it doesn't #performance","done":"2026-08-25T13:25:59-04:00","desc":"homes builds a fresh Set of every outline path and a fresh broken-files Map per call (ops query.ts:533-534), and the browser calls it once per fold/unfold. The doc block above it asserts \"neither half is a walk\" — the id half is indeed a lookup; the file half is O(all files) per click.\n\nFix shape: hold both structures with the snapshot they describe; fix the comment either way.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T13:25:59-04:00","custom":{"bundled-in":"perf-capture-paths"}}
{"id":"perf-didyoumean","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a3","title":"Every refused reference runs a similarity check against every id in the vault #performance","done":"2026-08-25T13:25:59-04:00","desc":"\"Did you mean…\" walks all ids per unresolved reference — per refused edit from a stale tab, per bad id in a broken file, per armed chat chip (refusals.ts:75, suggest.ts:24, edit.ts, context.ts:81). Partly admitted; harmless one at a time, unbounded when a stale tab replays a burst of refusals.\n\nFix shape: cap the candidates (e.g. by length bucket) or compute suggestions lazily, only when a person will actually see them.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T13:25:59-04:00","custom":{"bundled-in":"perf-capture-paths"}}
{"id":"sidx-implement","parent":"search-index","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: build the index, benchmark it, open the PR, then pause for the human's manual test #performance","done":"2026-08-24T20:12:59-04:00","desc":"Reached the agreed pause point at 20:12 (terminal a41b4aac, 1 hour 8 minutes): PR #374 at commit cca1b21.\n\nThe index is maintained incrementally from whatever a write touched, and keeps one matcher grammar — operators the index cannot serve filter the index's candidate set through the existing matcher. It is built lazily, once per process (114.1 ms on the benchmark corpus).\n\nBenchmark (1,000 outlines / 21,552 records / 200 documents / 0.77 MB of prose): typical queries 15 to 21 times faster (14.9 ms down to 0.87 ms), a miss 69 times faster, and an honest rule for declining — a query matching a crowd of 19,600 hits is answered by walking instead, at 0.9 times parity, because the index would take 22 ms there and be genuinely slower. Maintenance costs a median of 1.03 ms to rewrite a 21-record file, and 0.36 ms for a revision that changes nothing.\n\nLocal checks: typecheck 16 of 16, 3624 unit tests passing with none failing plus 77, end-to-end 917 of 7947 steps, all passing. CI 10 of 10 at commit cca1b21.\n\nFlagged rather than deferred: master's architecture.md describes the parked semantic-recall index (packages/server/src/recall/, a Recall seam in the ops layer) which is not in the tree — text from an unmerged branch, left for the orchestrator and the human.\n\nHolding for the human's manual test; the Grok and pi reviews start on their word.","created":"2026-08-24T19:02:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/374","terminal":"a41b4aac"}}
{"id":"sidx-review-grok","parent":"search-index","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the Grok agent (session split off the author's) — after the human's manual test #performance","done":"2026-08-24T20:28:27-04:00","desc":"The Grok agent did not object, with no blocking findings, at commit cca1b21 (20:28, 10 minutes 49 seconds, reviewing by reading the code, with one targeted probe into how FTS5's trigram matching compares with JavaScript substring matching): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/374#issuecomment-5403302722\n\nConcurrency was judged sound: the event loop serializes a write or a second query around the lazy first build.\n\nThree non-blocking notes, converging with the pi review: the soak test never crosses the crowd threshold at which the index declines; a few write shapes are traced rather than soaked; and the benchmark does not state memory use.","after":["sidx-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T19:02:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"79b380df"}}
{"id":"sidx-review-pi","parent":"search-index","ord":"a2","title":"Review by the pi agent (session split off the author's) — after the human's manual test #performance","done":"2026-08-24T20:26:39-04:00","desc":"The pi agent did not object, raising one recommendation and two minor notes, at commit cca1b21 (20:26, about 10 minutes, reviewing by reading — \"one targeted probe was allowed; none was needed\"): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/374#issuecomment-5403282539\n\nAll six axes were verified. Staleness is clean: the index is updated in the same table as the reading it answers from, so there is no window in which a publisher has moved on; every shape of write either rides the probe-stamp, codec-delta and patcher channel, or is derived state the matcher re-evaluates; and the one corpus where the candidate set and the walk could differ — duplicate ids — is the corpus the validator refuses. The differential test compares full answers including order, the withDesc flag, totals and caps, and asserts that candidates are a superset; two gaps (the soak never crosses the crowd threshold, and churn in mirrors is untested) were judged safe by construction. The rule for declining uses a bounded lookup — the crowd size plus one — against a measured threshold, and can only choose the safe path. There is one matcher, with no second spelling: edge cases fall towards over-inclusion or refusal, never a lost hit. Concurrency is fine: SQLite here is synchronous and single-threaded, so the first build is atomic.\n\nThe one real finding, a recommendation: memory is unstated. The brainstorm priced FTS5's trigram postings at roughly three times the text size on disk, the implementation moved them onto the long-lived Bun heap, and neither the benchmark nor the README says what that costs per record.\n\nMinor notes: the benchmark's \"why\" column re-states the trigram floor in UTF-16 units; and query.ts's doc comment attributes the soak test to the wrong thing.","after":["sidx-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T19:02:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"9f620892"}}
{"id":"sidx-close","parent":"search-index","ord":"a3","title":"Apply the reviews, run CI once, merge automatically on green (approved at 20:14) #performance","done":"2026-08-24T21:33:02-04:00","desc":"Closed at 21:34. All four points were addressed at commit 814e198c:\n1. Memory measured — 291 bytes per indexed record or document on the long-lived Bun heap, so the brainstorm's estimate of roughly three times the text size holds in RAM too — and stated in the benchmark, the README and the pull request description.\n2. The soak test now crosses the crowd threshold in both directions, in phases: the write mix is biased per phase, words of known selectivity are planted, both answers are compared every round, and each phase asserts that the decision flipped; the threshold constant is exported so the phases read the real value.\n3. The seven traced write shapes are now generated with non-zero counters, so a shape that stops being reachable fails the test rather than quietly shrinking coverage.\n4. Both minor notes.\n\nA regression the author caught themselves was fixed: the phased soak was five times slower than the repository's slowest test and timed out under the parallel suite; commit 814e198c makes the superset check a single assertion and states a 30-second hang backstop, bringing it back to about 3 seconds, green twice. Deliberate breakages prove the coverage bites — dropping branches makes the counts fail. Master was merged over a real conflict (4e089dc3, the ops row — the one condition CLAUDE.md names). Honesty about machine load: numbers from a quiet machine and from one at load average 37 are both stated, the ratios held, and the memory figure was byte-identical.\n\nLocal checks: typecheck 16 of 16, 3655 passing none failing plus 77, twice; CI 10 of 10 at 814e198c, end-to-end 930 of 8080 steps. Merged as 90460125 (squash) on the human's approval at 20:14 plus their automatic-merge word at 20:15; working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal a41b4aac retired.","after":["sidx-review-grok","sidx-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-24T19:02:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"pldb-implement","parent":"perf-list-documents-bytes","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: remember byte counts, add a differential test and benchmark, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-24T20:54:17-04:00","desc":"Reported at 20:52 (terminal 1d169bd6, 15 minutes 4 seconds): PR #375 at commit 665868fe. The listing now reports `entry.bytes`, remembered where the store holds the body, instead of calling bytesOf(entry.body) per document in query.ts.\n\nThere is a differential test over the test-suite corpus and over a generated multi-byte corpus, with the classic failure pinned: bytesOf(\"👋\") is 4, while .length is 2. Benchmark (5,000 documents, 5.77 MB, emoji in every seventh): 4.69 ms down to 0.79 ms, median of 9 runs, with the run failing unless both implementations answer identically — 5.9 times faster. End-to-end tests untouched, since the answers are byte-identical. The remaining per-call walk cost is named on the roadmap node and left out of scope.\n\nLocal checks: typecheck 15 of 15, 3651 unit tests passing with none failing plus 77. CI correctly deferred to the closing step. No deferred work.\n\nPull request at commit 665868fe.","created":"2026-08-24T20:37:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/375","terminal":"1d169bd6"}}
{"id":"pldb-review-pi","parent":"perf-list-documents-bytes","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the pi agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-24T20:58:51-04:00","desc":"The pi agent did not object, with no blocking findings, no recommendations and one minor note, at commit 665868fe (20:58, about 4 minutes, reviewing by reading): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/375#issuecomment-5403559273\n\nVerified with receipts. Staleness is clean: `bodiedDocument` (document.ts line 421) is the only place a Markdown document is constructed — checked by searching every kind — and writes (following.ts line 128), external edits and git pulls (codec.ts line 60, via the probe's re-read), creation and renaming all funnel through it, so the byte count is recomputed whenever the body is; and no serialized boundary decodes old Document values, so there is no migration hazard. There is one source of truth: bytesOf (documents.ts line 450) is the sole definition, with one call feeding the field. The differential test walks the real fixtures in the suite corpus, and the generated corpus covers astral-plane characters, zero-width joiners, combining marks, CJK text and frontmatter containing emoji; interaction with a half-written file is handled. The benchmark alternates warm-ups, takes a median of 9, and compares whole listings as JSON before timing anything.\n\nMinor note: the differential test covers a cold listing only — a write-then-relist case would pin the single-constructor property.","after":["pldb-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T20:37:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"8ed6a3a6"}}
{"id":"pldb-close","parent":"perf-list-documents-bytes","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-24T22:06:26-04:00","desc":"Closed at 22:07. The minor note was addressed with a write-then-relist differential test, and PR #374's conflict was then merged at commit 3b3e354f: `bodiedDocument` remains the only place a Markdown document is constructed — the index rebuild and the listing share one decode, and a write refreshes the byte count as it re-folds the body the index indexes — and #374's write-gate soak test now also asserts that the listing's sizes equal recomputed ones after every write, including write_document and create_document, so the two differential tests compose.\n\nAt the merged commit: typecheck 16 of 16, 3661 passing none failing plus 77, documents.feature 32 of 32. CI: the first run was 9 of 10 because of an unrelated end-to-end flake in the_conversations_servers.feature, which was recorded; the end-to-end re-run was 930 of 930 and all green. Merged as 0c71691b (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 1d169bd6 retired.","after":["pldb-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-24T20:37:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"fsc-implement","parent":"perf-filter-scope","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: narrow the scoped walk, build a reusable differential harness, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-24T22:21:34-04:00","desc":"Reported at 22:20 (terminal 7c8a30ac, 45 minutes 50 seconds): PR #380.\n\n`inScopeOf` now reads the derivation's own indexes: `file:` uses byFile (within a file, disk order is corpus order), and `under:` uses descendedFrom, which is ancestorsOf read backwards and inherits its stopping rules — placements are not descended through, placement and unclaimed roots are empty corners, and the cycle guard is symmetric. It composes with PR #374: the index narrows, the scope then tests the narrowed list at that list's cost, and neither reads the whole set — stated in the scoping code's header.\n\nThe harness, published as @olai/format/testlib/scope: the old walk kept as a reference implementation, plus a comparator checking set membership and then order, by query, position and record. Corpora: hand-written corner cases (a mirror trap, loops, cross-file, unclaimed, archived), a generated deep vault, the real docs/ vault, an edited vault (four writes against a patched view — the stale-children catch), and a case where the nodes getter throws, asserting the corpus is never read.\n\nIt earned its keep three times already: it caught the author's own duplicate-id fixture, naming 90 divergences precisely; it caught all four deliberate mutations; and it caught a bug in the loaders' suffix handling (commit 2e995499).\n\nBenchmark: `file:` 9.1 times faster, `under:` at the root 116 times, on a small subtree 1632 times, with a control at 1.0 (both implementations in the tree). Master was merged at commit baa3208b over a real conflict — PR #375's justfile and README steps. On the merged tree: typecheck clean, 3671 passing none failing plus 77 (10 new), and the touched end-to-end tests 74 of 563 green both before and after the merge. CI correctly held for the closing step.","created":"2026-08-24T21:33:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/380","terminal":"7c8a30ac"}}
{"id":"fsc-review-pi","parent":"perf-filter-scope","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the pi agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-24T22:38:44-04:00","desc":"The pi agent approved, with two recommendations and two minor notes, at commit 5a07615 (22:37, reviewing by reading — except that it also ran the odu CI itself, its second breach of the read-only review contract; the orchestrator's brief wording, \"gate: auto-merge iff harness green\", likely misled it into verifying the gate, and this is recorded on its roster node): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/380\n\nAxes settled: reading ancestors backwards is equivalent, with no asymmetry — the mirror stop, placement roots and cycle guard are symmetric by construction, and the sole class of divergence, duplicate ids, is refused by the validator and named in the header. Order is fine: byCorpus is the total order derived.nodes already promises, with the cross-file case pinned by the t-cross test. Staleness is covered by four writes with floors and moved witnesses, guarding against a vacuous test. Composition is proven: the matching code at line 2281 branches once, the withoutCorpus trap proves no branch reads the corpus, and declining to the walk is the only fallback and is itself scoped. Reusability holds: it is published as @olai/format/testlib/scope, with only the reference walk specific to this work. Zero divergences across the harness — 7 differential and 3 composition assertions green.\n\nRecommendations: the conjunction of an `under:` scope on a placement or missing node with a live `file:` scope is never asked (testlib line 482); and rounds 3 and 4 of the edited-vault test draw their queries from the pre-edit corpus (test line 300).\n\nMinor notes: the benchmark's selection guard only prints rather than asserting; and untrashing and cross-file moves deserve a written answer, both being provably unable to hide a node.","after":["fsc-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T21:33:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"9c434ab7"}}
{"id":"fsc-close","parent":"perf-filter-scope","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically if the harness is green #performance","done":"2026-08-24T22:55:18-04:00","desc":"Closed at 22:56. All four points landed at commit fa2659ea, with filter.ts byte-identical to the reviewed commit — the changes touched only the harness, tests, benchmark and justfile prose: the unasked conjunction was added and proven to do work by deliberately breaking it (1 of 10 cases red became 6 of 10); rounds 3 and 4 now draw from the patched view; the benchmark guard now asserts; and untrashing and cross-file moves are answered in the header comment — untrashing cannot hide a node, because archiving is decided during selection, downstream of candidates, and behaves the same in both implementations.\n\nLocal gates green. CI had one completed run at the merge commit; the author honestly superseded a run orphaned by the prose fix, and named the distinction between one dispatch and one run. The harness gate was met: zero divergences across all four corpora. Merged as 50530950 (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 7c8a30ac retired.","after":["fsc-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-24T21:33:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"pmb-implement","parent":"perf-published-maps","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: reuse the maps instead of rebuilding them, add the delta-stream equivalence harness, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-24T23:55:00-04:00","desc":"Reported at 23:53 (terminal 2ae28e09, 56 minutes 49 seconds): PR #382.\n\nPublishing now reuses map containers where a revision did not touch them; a membership change still rebuilds, which is stated and printed as the benchmark's middle row rather than omitted. The delta-stream equivalence harness landed: a fake subscriber replays the operation corpus against the old and new code and asserts an identical sequence of deltas and an identical final readAll; the hazard of a reused container swallowing deltas is proven catchable; there is an invalidation test and an allocation benchmark.\n\nLocal checks: typecheck clean, 3714 passing none failing across 267 files plus 77, full end-to-end 932 of 8104. CI correctly held for the closing step.\n\nAn honest observation was filed as its own node, phantom-sidebar-key-on-unnamed-remove: a file leaving in a revision the store cannot name stays visible to open readers until they reconnect — pre-existing byte for byte, but now measured by the harness, and a piece of work in its own right.\n\nThe session stopped for review correctly.","created":"2026-08-24T22:56:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/382","terminal":"2ae28e09"}}
{"id":"pmb-review-grok","parent":"perf-published-maps","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the Grok agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T00:06:04-04:00","desc":"The Grok agent objected, with one blocking finding, on PR #382 (00:05, 9 minutes 54 seconds, reviewing by reading): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/382#issuecomment-5404933393\n\nThe blocking finding, on correctness and aliasing: the reuse test treats directory size as each collection's membership, and the reuse path never deletes — so a named removal of one kind plus a birth of another kind, at constant file count, leaves the departed key in readAll for the life of the process. The reason it escaped is coverage: every revision in the corpus that changes membership does so within one kind, so the harness cannot see it.\n\nThe other axes were settled clean: the swallow mutant tests a capability rather than a product path, and the real subscriber's shape was verified; delta ordering is `moved.changed` in both implementations; the benchmark's middle row is an honest 1.0 times, meaning a genuine rebuild; and allocation counting patches Map.prototype.set, so writes into carried maps are visible.","after":["pmb-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T22:56:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"e4e27582"}}
{"id":"pmb-close","parent":"perf-published-maps","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically if the harness is green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T00:38:19-04:00","desc":"Closed at 00:39. The blocking finding was addressed on both halves at commit 4bb6eef1: a per-collection membership test, with a reuse path that deletes; and the corpus extended with membership changes that mix kinds. This was done red first, then green, as ordered — against the pre-fix commit bcc15008 the extended harness fails on all three corpora, naming the revision and the key, plus both unit assertions; after the fix it is 23 of 23. Incidentally, the harness and benchmark now read the kind registry instead of spelling out file suffixes.\n\nThe harness was green and the local checks were green (3716 passing none failing plus 77, end-to-end 932 of 8104), with CI 10 of 10 at commit 4bb6eef1. The author stopped correctly, noting \"the merge is the orchestrator's\". Merged as 21a22396 (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 2ae28e09 retired.","after":["pmb-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-24T22:56:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"vis-implement","parent":"perf-validate-incremental","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: the incremental validator as a shadow, with a divergence alarm and property test; open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T01:45:09-04:00","desc":"Reported at 01:44 (terminal f759fed5, 1 hour 4 minutes): PR #383.\n\nThe validator was narrowed as described in model-indices.md line 57, and ships as a shadow: both implementations run on every write, the full one stays authoritative, and a divergence is logged and reported at error level. The property test found zero divergences over 1,500 generated revisions (884 narrowed, 616 cold, 822 refused, 497 unreadable) plus 121 revisions from the real vault.\n\nBenchmark: a keystroke goes from 23.4 ms to 0.02 ms when narrowed; adding an edge honestly still runs all three walks; and the cost of running both implementations is stated — writes pay more until the switch-over. The switch-over is filed separately as perf-validate-flip: merged by the human, gated on an empty divergence log and at least three quiet nights, with a 7-day deadline that defaults to removal.\n\nTwo fixes the author caught themselves landed at commit b6149e09.\n\nTiming slip: CI was started at implementation time, although the brief said to run it at close, so the run may be superseded by review fixes — noted rather than interrupted. The Grok review was dispatched in parallel.","created":"2026-08-25T00:39:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/383","terminal":"f759fed5"}}
{"id":"vis-review-grok","parent":"perf-validate-incremental","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the Grok agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T01:57:53-04:00","desc":"The Grok agent objected — do not merge as it stands — with one blocking finding plus a recommendation and a minor note, on PR #383 (01:56, 10 minutes 40 seconds, reviewing by reading, with the read-only contract explicitly honoured): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/383#issuecomment-5406172956\n\nThe blocking finding: a fix commit renamed the divergence log the process actually writes ($XDG_STATE_HOME/olai/validate-shadow.ndjson), but the switch-over's machine-checkable gate still named the log by its old name with the banned extension — the pre-rename \".jsonl\" spelling — a file this process never writes. The gate would therefore read \"absent\" as \"empty\" and green-light the switch-over vacuously. This is the same incomplete-rename class of bug that extension.test.ts exists to catch, and which it cannot reach because roadmap files are exempt. (This note itself once quoted the old spelling verbatim and failed PR #383's own assertion on master; it was reworded on 2026-08-25 at 08:17 — the assertion polices even the board's prose, which is the point.)\n\nThe other axes were clean, with a table per rule: the duplicate-id rule asks the gate again; the parent and target rules re-ask from the maintained maps; the cycle rule runs whole or not at all, triggered by the edge tuple; and the document rule carries a complete delta keyed by path and file kind, explicitly checked as not being the membership-proxy shape PR #382 got wrong. The shadow cannot change control flow: it returns nothing, records the full ledger in a `finally`, turns a throw into `why: threw`, and shouts rather than rethrows if writing the log fails — all pinned by tests.\n\nMinor note: \"cold\" is one bucket covering first load, rebuild, and declining because of duplicate ids.","after":["vis-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T00:39:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"dc9ae548"}}
{"id":"vis-close","parent":"perf-validate-incremental","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T02:28:08-04:00","desc":"Closed at 02:28. The blocking finding was addressed at commit 479efb0b: the divergence log's path is now one exported constant shared by the writer and every mention of it, with a test pinning that, so the incomplete-rename class of bug cannot recur. The roadmap edit was stripped out of the pull request — verified as zero docs/roadmap files in the final diff, the first enforcement of the rule that work sessions do not write to the board. The Grok agent's recommendation, its cold-bucket minor note, and the parked documentation tweaks were all folded in.\n\nAt the folded commit: 3,737 tests passing with none failing plus 77, end-to-end 932 of 8104 with no divergence log written, and the benchmark honestly reporting both implementations. CI all green at 479efb0b. Merged as 3b51589b (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal f759fed5 retired. The shadow starts soaking tonight; the perf-validate-flip node, filed on the live board with the correct .ndjson path, carries the human-gated switch-over.","after":["vis-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-25T00:39:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-validate-flip","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a7","title":"Flip the incremental validator authoritative — one line, merge=HUMAN #performance","done":"2026-08-27T09:49:15-04:00","desc":"The narrowed validator runs as a SHADOW since #383. This is the one line in format/src/validate.ts that makes its verdict the answer, and the removal of the full arm from the write path.\n\n- Merge is a HUMAN's: behaviour is what changes here — the shadow PR's whole argument was that nothing did.\n- GATE, machine-checkable: $XDG_STATE_HOME/olai/validate-shadow.ndjson empty or absent (the writer's own exported constant), AND ≥3 quiet nights of soak. A non-empty log freezes the flip.\n- TIME-BOUNDED: flip or remove within 7 days of 2026-08-25; default on the deadline is REMOVE. Either way incremental.ts, its test/testlib and the bench STAY.\n- **Soak ledger: COMPLETE — the gate is MET.** Night 1 (08-24→25) QUIET; night 2 (25→26) QUIET; night 3 (26→27) QUIET — log absent, checked 08:23. The flip lane dispatched per the standing order (Opus author, roster reviewer, merge=HUMAN). NOTE: the session cron for this check has died with every deploy restart — the checks have been run by hand; reminders.olai is the durable home if this recurs.","created":"2026-08-25T01:58:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T18:44:05-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/pvf.md","dispatched":"2026-08-27T08:26:00-04:00","from":"the converged nightly plan (perf-sweep nightly-plan prop) + #383's shadow","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/56f5ac37","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/411","reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"75797a4c","worktree":".worktrees/validate-flip"}}
{"id":"pst-implement","parent":"perf-streams-per-tab","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: compute each answer once per revision, add the per-subscriber equivalence harness, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T09:20:21-04:00","desc":"Reported at 09:18 (terminal b0cde507, 1 hour 21 minutes): PR #387.\n\nA new module, @olai/ops/standing.ts, produces one answer per question — a member plus its request — per revision, shared across subscribers; anything genuinely per-tab stays the framework's business, and runtime.ts's shape is unchanged. A second module, @olai/format/tape.ts, provides the pre-check: a recording view tapes every key that was asked for, misses included, and a walk tapes the whole index; the next revision then asks whether any of them moved. It is conservative by construction, resting on the patcher's copy-on-write promise, and its limit is named honestly — a file that is re-decoded mints new record objects, so its questions rebuild; closing that gap is a store-level piece of work in its own right. One deliberate semantic is documented: the clock used for narrowing is sampled once per question per revision.\n\nHarness: 214 store-shaped revisions, 4 tabs, 16 questions, 3,424 asks — zero divergences, zero frame differences, and 731 answers built for 3,424 asks; 3 broken wirings and 5 per-member mutations all caught; and the negative space measured (0 wrong, 1,032 cautious).\n\nBenchmark: 2.57 ms down to 0.68 ms with one tab, 17.03 ms to 0.54 ms with ten, and the page answer at ten tabs from 10.83 ms to 0.07 ms; the tape's own cost, about 8% while typing, is stated.\n\nSuite 3,763 with 1 failure — the divergence sweep at this branch's starting point, already fixed on master at commit 9debbb7b; merging master was ordered. CI correctly left for the closing step. The session stopped correctly.","created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/387","terminal":"b0cde507"}}
{"id":"pst-review-grok","parent":"perf-streams-per-tab","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the Grok agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T09:32:00-04:00","desc":"The Grok agent did not object, but raised one blocking finding to be fixed before merge, with no recommendations and two minor notes, at commit 5f0da27f (09:31, 10 minutes 9 seconds, reviewing by reading and ignoring merge noise): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/387#issuecomment-5411113731\n\nThe blocking finding: the tape on the pull request's commit special-cases only Derived.nodes, but master's PR #386 added Derived.days, which is an array — and the INDEXES list is built with Object.keys, which picks up maps, not lists. So the taped view's `days` is undefined and `dayAt` throws on every calendar or agenda rebuild; and forwarding it without taping it would be the same class of miss as PR #382's (a check that misses a whole category). The merge already in flight in the working tree closes it, with LISTS and carriedList, but that close must be part of the fold on the head commit.\n\nAxes: the sharing seam is clean — pollOnEvent compares by value rather than identity, and a standing answer is never mutated in place, since it either hands back the previous answer or a fresh one; PR #382's failure shape was checked explicitly. Taping absence covers misses, empty buckets and walks, and untaped reads do not typecheck. The clock is a named trade rather than a defect: the new case — a second tab joining after midnight inherits the first tab's yesterday-clock until the next revision — is documented, and no midnight pulse existed before either. Lifetime is bounded by questions continuing to be asked, over two reading rolls. The corpus really is store-shaped, driven by Since, and the re-read path did fire.\n\nMinor notes: the wording about eviction (it is two reading rolls, not asks); and a count of eleven out of thirteen that is out of date after PR #386.","after":["pst-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"b9c97d5e"}}
{"id":"pst-close","parent":"perf-streams-per-tab","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T10:05:44-04:00","desc":"Closed at 10:03, delayed about 15 minutes by a GitHub outage — the checks were verified green at commit 3d20f55 throughout. The blocking finding about lists landed, both minor notes were folded in, and the sweep was green on the merged tree. CI ran at the folded commit and over-ran onto both platforms against the Linux-only rule — harmless, and self-noted. The post-merge benchmark was honestly restated: the page answer at ten tabs goes from 11.19 ms to 0.08 ms, and a small net loss on the overdue answer is stated and kept uniform. Merged as 09fbd710 (squash); working tree and branch cleaned up, terminal b0cde507 retired.","after":["pst-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"pgw-implement","parent":"perf-git-per-write","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: cache HEAD's copy per commit and file, make the detailed diff lazy, add the scripted-session test, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T08:33:07-04:00","desc":"Reported at 08:32 (terminal 81a41d7a, 35 minutes 9 seconds): PR #385.\n\nThe cache is keyed by commit and file, with staleness impossible by construction. The scripted-session differential test keeps the old read as its reference implementation — the `forgetful` helper in committed.testlib.ts — and asserts the whole published answer at every step: a keystroke, a newly dirty file, an edit made outside olai, a commit, an amend, `reset --soft` to an already-seen commit, checking out away and back, a rename within the served set, a .md file renamed into the format, breaking and mending a file, and a file leaving the disk — at the repository root and in a served subdirectory, plus a repository with no commits at all. Subprocess counts are asserted exactly: with 50 files waiting, a keystroke costs 1; a newly dirty file costs 2; after a commit, files still dirty are read once each.\n\nBenchmark with 1, 10 and 50 dirty files: 1.1, 1.9 and 5.1 times faster (37.33 ms down to 7.36 ms at 50); with 20,000 dirty nodes, 181 ms down to 38 ms, with the remaining cost named — changesOf, which is cross-file by design.\n\nSuite 3757 passing none failing (16 new). Master was merged legitimately, since this branch started before the board-prose fix at commit 9debbb7b that divergenceLog.test.ts needs. Documentation moved with the claim, including an \"amended by\" note on the brainstorm sentence that is now wrong; no board files were touched. CI at close. The session stopped correctly.","created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/385","terminal":"81a41d7a"}}
{"id":"pgw-review-pi","parent":"perf-git-per-write","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the pi agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T08:49:13-04:00","desc":"The pi agent raised no blocking findings, one recommendation and two minor notes, on PR #385 (08:48, about 14 minutes, reviewing by reading plus one well-spent probe into git show's exit codes for an absent path versus an absent commit) — posted on the pull request.\n\nAxes: the cache key holds — a full-SHA `rev-parse` feeds `show`, and because the index and working tree are not inputs to the cache, the corners it misses cannot make the two implementations disagree; one `rev-parse` per revision is strictly more coherent than master's per-file fan-out. Eviction is one generation, replaced wholesale. The subprocess counter sits at the asking seam over the only owner of spawning, so no hidden spawn is possible. The reference implementation is cleanly test-only. The `reset --soft` case is covered by comparing the whole status at each step.\n\nThe recommendation, a real robustness regression: a transient failure of `git show` — a hung budget, for instance — folds to null and is then memoized as \"absent\" for the whole generation, where the old per-revision read healed itself. The probe confirmed the failure is classifiable, so an answer-derived null should be refused rather than filed.\n\nMinor notes: document why index-only corners cannot diverge; and one wording adjacency.","after":["pgw-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"508a665c"}}
{"id":"pgw-close","parent":"perf-git-per-write","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T09:08:39-04:00","desc":"Closed at 09:08. All three points landed at commit 8ca119b6.\n\nThe recommendation was taken, with the filing rule reasoned out: both implementations must be the same function of what git said, so a classifiably unusable answer reads as \"absent\" for that revision, just as the old fold did — but transient failures are no longer memoized across the generation. This is pinned in git.test.ts in both spellings, plus two cases in committed.test.ts, including one where a single unanswerable file does not stop the round. The index corners were scripted (restored out of HEAD, out of an older commit, staged then edited), with the derivation stated: `show` reads the object store. The wording about counts stopped quoting numbers and now points at the layer that spends them.\n\nSuite 3755 passing none failing (4 new). Honest note on the benchmark: the subprocess counts are the reproducible half — 50 down to 1, exactly — while the millisecond figures move with machine load (71 ms to 11 ms on a loaded re-run, same shape); the README says which half to trust. CI verified at the folded commit, 10 of 10. Merged as 74c71eab (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 81a41d7a retired.","after":["pgw-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"pah-implement","parent":"perf-agenda-history-walk","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: maintained overdue counts, sorted day lookup, differential test, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T08:41:25-04:00","desc":"Reported at 08:39 (terminal 0441313c, 42 minutes 56 seconds): PR #386 at commit 0bbaa88.\n\n`owedByDay` and `owedNow` are now indexes maintained by the patcher, and day lookups jump using a sorted list. Boundary cases are pinned: a bare day \"D\" sorts before \"DT09:00\"; a task marked done today stays in byDay with its instant but leaves owedByDay; archived and trashed nodes are pinned too.\n\nDifferential test: 300 generated corpora, both derived and patched, with the old walk as the reference implementation, plus ops/src/owed.index.test.ts against a real store — 22 writes chosen for their effect on overdueness, both spellings asked at 14 different values of \"today\", and the calendar asked across 5 months; the patcher's oracle test grew both new fields. Five deliberate mutations were all caught.\n\n`unfinishedWork` was extracted as the single predicate for what counts as owed, shared by the count and the page, and handed to the brief for the cancelled-mark work.\n\nBenchmark: the counts roughly 100 times faster (2 ms to 0.02 ms), the calendar month stepped rather than walked, the agenda page honestly a wash, and the per-write cost honestly a trade (about 0.4 ms more per rebuild). Suite 3744 passing none failing; the four gate suites 76 tests / 20,348 assertions.\n\nTiming slip: CI was run at implementation time, 10 of 10 at commit 0bbaa88, and will be superseded if the review moves the head commit. The session stopped correctly.\n\nPull request at commit 0bbaa88.","created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/386","terminal":"0441313c"}}
{"id":"pah-review-grok","parent":"perf-agenda-history-walk","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the Grok agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T08:51:16-04:00","desc":"The Grok agent did not object, with no blocking findings, one recommendation and one minor note, at commit 0bbaa88f (08:50, 8 minutes 56 seconds, reviewing by reading, with no probe needed): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/386#issuecomment-5410612919\n\nCompleteness of the index maintenance was settled with a table by class: the 22 writes are shapes of overdueness rather than a list of verbs, and the test says so; merging and splitting are covered structurally, because the patcher re-files `dateInto` over the touched files' records and never sees the verb; and `set_after` correctly does not change overdueness, since a blocked dated task stays on the agenda — which is pinned. The five mutations are mutations of the index and of the read, the sorted jump included.\n\nThe recommendation: `unfinishedWork` is composed as unfinished(storedMarker(node)), and \"unfinished\" today means neither undefined nor done — so a fourth mark added to MARKS would pass both tests, and dated cancelled work would stay owed. The extraction exists precisely to make that a one-place change, and its contract must say so; the cancelled-mark work's seam is named.\n\nMinor note: owed.index.test.ts line 203's \"NEW dated work\" is an unmarked occurrence — rename it, or give it mark: todo.","after":["pah-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"235278e4"}}
{"id":"pah-close","parent":"perf-agenda-history-walk","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T09:02:25-04:00","desc":"Closed at 09:02. Both points landed at commit 391ceea6: `unfinishedWork`'s header comment now names the cancelled-mark seam — the two readings, and why one composition — and node.ts's `Unfinished` gained a pointer back, so the pair is found together. The minor note was split into its two true claims: a dated occurrence mints byDay and days keys but none in owedByDay, and the tally's key arrives one write after the journal's. CI was verified at the folded commit (logs under .ci/391ceea, 10 of 10). Merged as e914a6c2 (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 0441313c retired.","after":["pah-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-25T07:56:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"pkr-implement","parent":"perf-key-resort","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: splice instead of re-sorting across the keyed indexes, differential test, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T09:44:14-04:00","desc":"Reported at 09:43 (terminal 495cce18, 33 minutes 48 seconds): PR #389.\n\nSplicing replaces the rebuild across the keyed indexes, with the old rebuild kept verbatim as the reference implementation. The differential test covers 7,918 keys harvested from real edits over generated corpora and over docs/, comparing set membership and order per key, with arrivals both as filed and shuffled, and with corner cases counted and floored: 493 born, 383 emptied, 6,923 mixed, 1,185 at the head, 1,137 at the tail, 2,760 cross-file.\n\nPlacing an element one position early and one position late are both caught, and the \"insert before its ties\" mutant was proven unreachable — ties imply a single file, and a survivor and an arrival never share one — which is asserted, and pinned anyway with hand-written tying keys under a comparator where everything ties.\n\nThe benchmark is honest: the gap widens with key size (32 µs down to 16 µs at 1,188 members), the null result for children (all in one file) is named, and the whole-patch median sits inside the noise, so the A/B of that one step is the figure to quote; slice-based variants measured within 10%, so the simple loop stayed.\n\nSuite 3,778 passing none failing, typecheck 16 of 16, and architecture.md's patcher bullet updated. No CI, no self-review — the session stopped correctly.","created":"2026-08-25T09:08:39-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/389","terminal":"495cce18"}}
{"id":"pkr-review-pi","parent":"perf-key-resort","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the pi agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T10:10:47-04:00","desc":"The pi agent did not object apart from one blocking finding, plus one recommendation and three minor notes, on PR #389 (10:10, about 25 minutes, reviewing by reading): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/389#issuecomment-5411624971\n\nAll four axes clear: the unreachability claim is structurally true and stronger than claimed — every ordering ends in byCorpus, whose ties imply the same record, and placeFor's past-the-equals placement matches the rebuild's stable sort for any pattern of ties; the comparator is one shared instance, with the children's two spellings proven equivalent and chained to the oracle; duplicate identity behaves identically in both implementations; and copy-on-write was traced through every return path.\n\nThe blocking finding: splice.test.ts line 357 keys its decode cache with a literal NUL byte at offset 16,772 — found because ripgrep refused the pull request's own gate file as binary. Git still sniffs it as text, but one more block of documentation would tip it into being binary, which is the shape of the earlier folds.ts incident. The fix is one character: escape it as \\0.\n\nThe recommendation, forward-looking and handed to the doc-backlinks work: the tie-floor assertion watches only the four hand-named indexes, so a new keyed index — doc-backlinks, being implemented right now — compiles into `refiled` with no tie accounting at all; the registration rule belongs in Filing.order's documentation (patch.ts lines 364-367).\n\nMinor notes: where byOrd's documentation places the line tiebreak; two different quoted figures for one benchmark step; and the shape of `owing`, where a negative tally would silently delete.","after":["pkr-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T09:08:39-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"ed3de9fa"}}
{"id":"pkr-close","parent":"perf-key-resort","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T10:22:06-04:00","desc":"Closed at 10:22. All the fixes landed at commit 5b85e229: the NUL byte was escaped as \\0, the one-character blocking fix; and the registration rule was written into Filing.order's documentation — a new client of `refiled` registers its (at, order) pair in casesFor and declares its ties, so an ordering that can tie a survivor with an arrival trips the tie floor once registered, and reorders silently if it never is, with perf-doc-backlinks-index named as the first customer. The minor notes were addressed: byOrd's tiebreak documentation corrected, the benchmark figure single-sourced, and `owing`'s negative-tally shape documented at the line, with the reason.\n\nMaster was merged in, including PR #387's tape and standing modules, after which the format suite was 892 passing with none failing. CI at the folded commit was 10 of 10. Merged as 73a63a10 (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 495cce18 retired.","after":["pkr-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-25T09:08:39-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"pdb-implement","parent":"perf-doc-backlinks-index","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: the links-to-documents index, differential test, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T10:50:53-04:00","desc":"Reported at 10:49 (terminal 510427f4, 43 minutes 24 seconds): PR #393.\n\nThe links-to-documents index is maintained by the patcher, with the old scan kept as the reference implementation. Seven deliberate mutations were all caught, including the subtle one where every file is counted as moved — correct, but never carried — which only the carried-by-reference case catches.\n\nThe tape's exhaustiveness was made structural: BESIDE is defined as everything in Reading except the set and the derivation, so a table added beside the derivation is a typecheck failure rather than an untaped read, which hardens PR #387's claim. PR #389's registration rule is argued not applicable in the header comment, this index being keyed by document, by path, with unique paths and therefore no survivor/arrival ties.\n\nThe benchmark is honest: 7.4 times faster at median link density, 190 times where nothing points at the document, and the trade named — the ratio rises with vault size and falls with link density, and the first load pays one fold of about 3.9 ms.\n\nSuite 3807 passing none failing (8 new), typecheck clean. CI correctly left for the closing step. The session stopped correctly.","created":"2026-08-25T10:05:44-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/393","terminal":"510427f4"}}
{"id":"pdb-review-grok","parent":"perf-doc-backlinks-index","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the Grok agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T11:05:01-04:00","desc":"The Grok agent said to fold the notes in and merge, with no blocking findings, no recommendations and three minor notes, at commit db19db72 (11:03, 11 minutes 24 seconds, reviewing by reading, with no probe needed) — posted on PR #393.\n\nAll five axes held: the index has the same membership as the scan, being keyed on the same printAddress the display already deduplicates by; the carry is `pointInto` on both sides of a path-ordered merge; the BESIDE type really is the typecheck fence it claims to be; a renamed or dead target is a written address rather than a phantom; and the first-load fold sits beside the first derive on the store's gated fiber, so it cannot interleave with a write.\n\nMinor notes: run-together possessives (page.ts line 297); pointing.ts line 282 passes the whole Document where faceOf's projection is what the index points at; and tape.ts line 105's `tableIn` special-cases pointing instead of using a Record over BESIDE.","after":["pdb-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T10:05:44-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"afd73fbe"}}
{"id":"pdb-close","parent":"perf-doc-backlinks-index","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T11:37:38-04:00","desc":"Closed at 11:38. The three minor notes were folded in at commit fbc18199: possessives fixed; pointInto takes faceOf's projection; and tableIn became a Record over BESIDE, filling in the gap.\n\nThere were three CI runs, all named honestly: the first was red on the author's own file-kind sweep catching a bare \".olai\" extension parameter — exactly the shape the sweep exists to find, missed locally because `git ls-files` does not see an untracked file; the second was red on the already-recorded phone flake, which PR #394 fixed between the two runs; and the third, at commit fbc1819, was 10 of 10.\n\nThe benchmark re-ran with the same shape: reads 2.72 ms down to 0.42 ms over 10 pages; writes 4.33 ms down to 0.115 ms to carry. Merged as 52ba0f81 (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 510427f4 retired.","after":["pdb-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-25T10:05:44-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"poc-implement","parent":"perf-overlay-copies","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: copy only on the first write, add the aliasing assertion, open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T10:47:10-04:00","desc":"Reported at 10:46 (terminal 9e748eb4, 22 minutes 11 seconds): PR #392.\n\nThe layered indexes now copy lazily, on the first write. The aliasing assertion grew by 5 cases, including one where an entry is dropped. All ten existing gate suites are green without modification, with individual counts: splice 7, patch 22, occasion/agenda, owed 1, tape 10, standing 11 plus 7, pending 4, derive 73, incremental 3; the format package 897 (5 new), the whole tree 3811 passing with none failing, typecheck 16 of 16.\n\nThe benchmark is honest: allocation counts are exact, measured with heapStats over retained rounds — 1 map and 2 sets versus 0 and 0, per index per patch, at the half-grown ceiling — while the end-to-end patch timing is inside the noise on this vault when compared against a variant with eager copying restored, because the indexes being skipped are the small ones here. What this removes is the per-index ceiling and the allocation under it, stated in the pull request description and architecture.md rather than dressed up.\n\nThe session stopped correctly.","created":"2026-08-25T10:23:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/392","terminal":"9e748eb4"}}
{"id":"poc-review-pi","parent":"perf-overlay-copies","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the pi agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T11:05:01-04:00","desc":"The pi agent approved, with one recommendation and one minor note, on PR #392 (11:02, reviewing by reading plus one probe that was verified by reverting it: making `own()` share the carried layer instead of copying produced 7 failures, including the named dropping assertion — so \"proven by mutation, not lucky\" is real).\n\nAll axes clean: every door that mutates copies first (set, delete, sealed; PR #389's splice reaches the indexes only through set and delete; there is no unnamed writer); copying while iterating is safe, since the fields are never reassigned and abandoned originals are never written to again; and the flatten boundary returns what it was given before the half check, with the carried size frozen.\n\nRecommendation: the benchmark's eager-copy fence cannot fail by construction, since eager()[0] is literally new Map(changed) — replace it with an observable check that the layer's size equals the base's.\n\nMinor note: the scope of the +5 / +6 catch counts.","after":["poc-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T10:23:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"b4b64d7e"}}
{"id":"poc-close","parent":"perf-overlay-copies","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green #performance","done":"2026-08-25T11:15:54-04:00","desc":"Closed at 11:16. The recommendation was folded in at commit ab15b1d2: the eager-copy fence was replaced with the observable size check, which now actually catches the failure; and the count note was scoped honestly. Master merged cleanly (PR #391's cancelled mark; the format suite went from 897 to 904, all green). CI 10 of 10 at commit ab15b1d — the kolu-ci-4 runner was unreachable and the pool moved itself to ci-5, which is not a failure of this work. Merged as bc886435 (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 9e748eb4 retired.","after":["poc-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-25T10:23:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
{"id":"pob-implement","parent":"perf-capture-paths","ord":"a0","title":"Implementation: remove the four ops-layer whole-vault walks, with reference implementations; open the PR #performance","done":"2026-08-25T12:43:24-04:00","desc":"Reported at 12:42 (terminal 7b66cf7f, 1 hour 24 minutes): PR #396. All four costs are addressed, each with its own equality and count gate:\n- capture now asks a paths-only question (718 times faster, and the retry's double payment is gone).\n- The untangling, per the re-scope: a PlanningContext computed once per batch, with pure `(context, operation) → plan` planners dispatched through a map. The old and new code produce byte-equal results over a scripted corpus covering every operation, and \"one asking per batch\" is asserted as an identity check. End to end a batch is honestly only 1.1 to 1.2 times faster, with the 7.8 times gap in set-building printed — a fold operation is four things and this changed one.\n- `homes` is now held (a fold click 15 times faster).\n- \"Did you mean\" is bounded, with the quality gate asserted over every existing fixture — and the differential test caught a live tie-break bug during development, an admission at budget plus one, verified with a probe and then reverted.\n\nTwo honest deviations are flagged in the code: path lookup stays a binary search, because documentAt has been one since PR #382 and the brief's map idea was out of date — what is held is the broken-files map rebuild; and the remaining per-operation whole-vault cost is reading()'s check against the patched view, which is out of scope and filed as the perf-reading-patched-check node.\n\nSuite 3851 passing none failing plus 77, typecheck clean, documentation updated across four files. CI correctly at close. The session stopped correctly.","created":"2026-08-25T11:17:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/396","terminal":"7b66cf7f"}}
{"id":"pob-review-pi","parent":"perf-capture-paths","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the pi agent (session split off the author's) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T13:02:34-04:00","desc":"The pi agent approved, with no blocking findings, one recommendation, five minor notes and one operational note, on PR #396 (13:02, about 18 minutes, reviewing by reading plus one probe): https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/396#issuecomment-5413876872\n\nAxes: the dispatch is structurally guarded, because PLANNERS is keyed on Request[op], so a missing verb is a compile error, and the golden test asserts all 26 operation kinds across the 63-step script with exact counts. Freshness is exact: the mint and mend decline rules hold, a fresh Map is used per operation per PR #392's rule, and the equivalence fold pins the set, the broken map, the view, the plans and the refusals per operation. Purity was established by exhaustive search — everything routes through scope.asked. The probe reconstructed the shape of the bug that was caught (a first-map candidate at budget plus one), proving suggest.ts line 205 is load-bearing, with 3,000 dense queries producing zero disagreements between the doors. Both flagged deviations are honest: documentAt read as a binary search, and the patched-view check named and not touched.\n\nRecommendation: plans.testlib.ts lines 22-24's sentence about coverage is false — todo, cancelled, desc, date and repeat never refuse, and split appears exactly once, which is the brief's own watch-list verb.\n\nMinor notes: dead imports, a dangling link, and the declared door's boundary appearing only in the seeded differential test.\n\nOperational note: the branch predates PR #393, so the justfile will conflict at merge; the resolution is thirteen steps, both paragraphs, with the shared-vault count recounted.","after":["pob-implement"],"created":"2026-08-25T11:17:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"4796dfe2"}}
{"id":"pob-close","parent":"perf-capture-paths","ord":"a2","title":"Apply the review, run CI once, merge automatically on green (closes all four bundled nodes) #performance","done":"2026-08-25T13:25:59-04:00","desc":"Closed at 13:26. The recommendation was folded in at commit 24e79084 — the corpus grew to include refusing cases where refusals exist, and the sentence about coverage now tells the truth — the minor notes were folded in, and the justfile conflict was resolved to thirteen steps, as the reviewer's operational note described. CI 10 of 10 at commit 24e7908, with GitHub reporting the branch clean. Merged as afc093d8 (squash); working tree removed, branch deleted, terminal 7b66cf7f retired.\n\nAll four bundled nodes close with this — the dispatchable catalogue of the whole-vault-cost sweep is done: every entry is merged or waiting on the human, the one exception being the validator switch-over, which is soaking.","after":["pob-review-pi"],"created":"2026-08-25T11:17:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:16:44-04:00"}
